INSERT INTO sat_questions (question_id, external_id, vault_id, disclosed_item_id, source, program, test_type, test_type_desc, domain, primary_class_cd, skill_cd, skill_desc, difficulty, score_band_range_cd, question_type, stimulus_html, stem_html, answer_options, correct_answer, answer_keys, rationale_html, correct_answer_extracted_from_rationale, image_count, parent_template_name, parent_template_id, template_cluster_id, template_cluster_name, origin, position, answer_style, section, raw_payload, created_date, updated_date) VALUES
    ($SATQ$31d0bd9a$SATQ$, $SATQ$20fbf413-7cdc-4095-9049-97cfda456a48$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$e1ccd171-70d1-415c-b72e-b743462299d3$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Words in Context$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 3, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The parasitic dodder plant increases its reproductive success by flowering at the same time as the host plant it has latched onto. In 2020, Jianqiang Wu and his colleagues determined that the tiny dodder achieves this <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> with its host by absorbing and utilizing a protein the host produces when it is about to flower.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"d0bfb104-4354-4428-980c-3b660a59bf98","content":"<p><strong> </strong>synchronization</p>"},{"id":"08aa1c56-cdcc-4945-8908-8b9146413216","content":"<p>hibernation</p>"},{"id":"fa679281-cefb-4740-bf63-548ac35ac857","content":"<p>prediction</p>"},{"id":"92488a29-7b12-4358-89b7-ad886cf85b69","content":"<p>moderation</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["d0bfb104-4354-4428-980c-3b660a59bf98"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of a relationship between the dodder plant and its host plant. As used in this context, &ldquo;synchronization&rdquo; means the act of things happening at the same time. The text indicates that the dodder and its host plant flower in unison and that this synchronization occurs because the dodder makes use of a protein produced by the host shortly before flowering. </p><p>Choice B is incorrect because referring to &ldquo;hibernation,&rdquo; or the state of being dormant or inactive, wouldn&rsquo;t make sense in context. The text focuses on something the dodder plant actively engages in&mdash;making use of a protein and producing flowers. Choice C is incorrect because stating that the dodder plant and its host engage together in &ldquo;prediction,&rdquo; or the act of declaring or indicating something in advance, wouldn&rsquo;t make sense in context. Rather than indicating that the dodder plant and its host plant make a prediction about flowering activity, the text suggests that the host produces a protein as part of its regular flowering process and that the dodder then absorbs and uses that protein to flower at the same time. Choice D is incorrect because referring to &ldquo;moderation,&rdquo; or the act of causing something to become less intense or extreme, wouldn&rsquo;t make sense in context. Although the text states that the dodder plant absorbs and uses a protein made by its host plant, it doesn&rsquo;t suggest that the dodder lessens the host plant&rsquo;s flowering activity; the two plants simply flower in unison. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-903-WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$f9207e9e-ead6-415d-8341-d79c49a347aa$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"31d0bd9a","external_id":"20fbf413-7cdc-4095-9049-97cfda456a48","vaultid":"e1ccd171-70d1-415c-b72e-b743462299d3","uId":"886cc91b-b595-412a-9321-d9b533c4158a","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"WIC","skill_desc":"Words in Context","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":3,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>The parasitic dodder plant increases its reproductive success by flowering at the same time as the host plant it has latched onto. In 2020, Jianqiang Wu and his colleagues determined that the tiny dodder achieves this <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> with its host by absorbing and utilizing a protein the host produces when it is about to flower.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"d0bfb104-4354-4428-980c-3b660a59bf98","content":"<p><strong> </strong>synchronization</p>"},{"id":"08aa1c56-cdcc-4945-8908-8b9146413216","content":"<p>hibernation</p>"},{"id":"fa679281-cefb-4740-bf63-548ac35ac857","content":"<p>prediction</p>"},{"id":"92488a29-7b12-4358-89b7-ad886cf85b69","content":"<p>moderation</p>"}],"keys":["d0bfb104-4354-4428-980c-3b660a59bf98"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of a relationship between the dodder plant and its host plant. As used in this context, &ldquo;synchronization&rdquo; means the act of things happening at the same time. The text indicates that the dodder and its host plant flower in unison and that this synchronization occurs because the dodder makes use of a protein produced by the host shortly before flowering. </p><p>Choice B is incorrect because referring to &ldquo;hibernation,&rdquo; or the state of being dormant or inactive, wouldn&rsquo;t make sense in context. The text focuses on something the dodder plant actively engages in&mdash;making use of a protein and producing flowers. Choice C is incorrect because stating that the dodder plant and its host engage together in &ldquo;prediction,&rdquo; or the act of declaring or indicating something in advance, wouldn&rsquo;t make sense in context. Rather than indicating that the dodder plant and its host plant make a prediction about flowering activity, the text suggests that the host produces a protein as part of its regular flowering process and that the dodder then absorbs and uses that protein to flower at the same time. Choice D is incorrect because referring to &ldquo;moderation,&rdquo; or the act of causing something to become less intense or extreme, wouldn&rsquo;t make sense in context. Although the text states that the dodder plant absorbs and uses a protein made by its host plant, it doesn&rsquo;t suggest that the dodder lessens the host plant&rsquo;s flowering activity; the two plants simply flower in unison. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-903-WIC","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"f9207e9e-ead6-415d-8341-d79c49a347aa","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959354,"updateDate":1691007959354}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959354, 1691007959354),
    ($SATQ$34d7bb25$SATQ$, $SATQ$a83de30e-b2fc-4be2-9fdc-19216a085296$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$4ebb7580-017b-459d-99a2-6a0b0fc48203$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$TSP$SATQ$, $SATQ$Text Structure and Purpose$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 6, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>According to Indian economist and sociologist Radhakamal Mukerjee (1889&ndash;1968), the Eurocentric concepts that informed early twentieth-century social scientific methods&mdash;for example, the idea that all social relations are reducible to struggles between individuals&mdash;had little relevance for India. Making the social sciences more responsive to Indians&rsquo; needs, Mukerjee argued, required constructing analytical categories informed by India&rsquo;s cultural and ecological circumstances. Mukerjee thus proposed the communalist &ldquo;Indian village&rdquo; as the ideal model on which to base Indian economic and social policy.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"94984ae8-0856-4568-aca4-c55fd41adebd","content":"<p>The text recounts Mukerjee&rsquo;s early training in the social scientific disciplines and then lists social policies whose implementation Mukerjee oversaw.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"fea3fd3a-9a1e-4807-bf52-42b77503fe13","content":"<p>The text mentions some of Mukerjee&rsquo;s economic theories and then traces their impact on other Indian social scientists of the twentieth century.</p>"},{"id":"caaf677e-4d85-485f-bdf6-8da2ff01a0dd","content":"<p>The text presents Mukerjee&rsquo;s critique of the social sciences and then provides an example of his attempts to address issues he identified in his critique.</p>"},{"id":"599acb26-424c-412b-88a4-a96ed65d4272","content":"<p>The text explains an influential economic theory and then demonstrates how that theory was more important to Mukerjee&rsquo;s work than other social scientists have acknowledged. &nbsp;</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["caaf677e-4d85-485f-bdf6-8da2ff01a0dd"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer. The text does indeed present Mukerjee&rsquo;s critique of the social sciences&mdash;that they were too Eurocentric&mdash;and then provides an example of how he attempted to address the issues he identified: by suggesting a social science model based on the Indian village. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. The text doesn&rsquo;t discuss Mukerjee&rsquo;s early training or his oversight of the implementation of social policies at all. Choice B is incorrect. The text never discusses any other Indian social scientists. Choice D is incorrect. The text never mentions other social scientists&rsquo; responses to Mukerjee&rsquo;s work. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-KA-TSP-H008$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$24e4a9aa-4cf8-4a3c-acca-b72c403bb66f$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"34d7bb25","external_id":"a83de30e-b2fc-4be2-9fdc-19216a085296","vaultid":"4ebb7580-017b-459d-99a2-6a0b0fc48203","uId":"8e2a723f-b84f-4b55-8bb7-88822941ec52","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"TSP","skill_desc":"Text Structure and Purpose","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":6,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>According to Indian economist and sociologist Radhakamal Mukerjee (1889&ndash;1968), the Eurocentric concepts that informed early twentieth-century social scientific methods&mdash;for example, the idea that all social relations are reducible to struggles between individuals&mdash;had little relevance for India. Making the social sciences more responsive to Indians&rsquo; needs, Mukerjee argued, required constructing analytical categories informed by India&rsquo;s cultural and ecological circumstances. Mukerjee thus proposed the communalist &ldquo;Indian village&rdquo; as the ideal model on which to base Indian economic and social policy.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"94984ae8-0856-4568-aca4-c55fd41adebd","content":"<p>The text recounts Mukerjee&rsquo;s early training in the social scientific disciplines and then lists social policies whose implementation Mukerjee oversaw.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"fea3fd3a-9a1e-4807-bf52-42b77503fe13","content":"<p>The text mentions some of Mukerjee&rsquo;s economic theories and then traces their impact on other Indian social scientists of the twentieth century.</p>"},{"id":"caaf677e-4d85-485f-bdf6-8da2ff01a0dd","content":"<p>The text presents Mukerjee&rsquo;s critique of the social sciences and then provides an example of his attempts to address issues he identified in his critique.</p>"},{"id":"599acb26-424c-412b-88a4-a96ed65d4272","content":"<p>The text explains an influential economic theory and then demonstrates how that theory was more important to Mukerjee&rsquo;s work than other social scientists have acknowledged. &nbsp;</p>"}],"keys":["caaf677e-4d85-485f-bdf6-8da2ff01a0dd"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer. The text does indeed present Mukerjee&rsquo;s critique of the social sciences&mdash;that they were too Eurocentric&mdash;and then provides an example of how he attempted to address the issues he identified: by suggesting a social science model based on the Indian village. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. The text doesn&rsquo;t discuss Mukerjee&rsquo;s early training or his oversight of the implementation of social policies at all. Choice B is incorrect. The text never discusses any other Indian social scientists. Choice D is incorrect. The text never mentions other social scientists&rsquo; responses to Mukerjee&rsquo;s work. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-KA-TSP-H008","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"24e4a9aa-4cf8-4a3c-acca-b72c403bb66f","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959375,"updateDate":1691007959375}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959375, 1691007959375),
    ($SATQ$3566120b$SATQ$, $SATQ$9d21eb26-86d3-4193-9e31-607f67f13f4e$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$052c1e78-31ab-40e0-a83b-45174447078d$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Words in Context$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 6, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The following text is adapted from Oscar Wilde&rsquo;s 1895 play <em>The Importance of Being Earnest</em>.</p>
<p style="padding-left:1em">CECILY: Have we got to part?</p>
<p style="padding-left:1em">ALGERNON: I am afraid so. It&rsquo;s a very painful parting.</p>
<p style="padding-left:1em"s>CECILY: It is always painful to part from people whom one has known for a very brief space of time. The absence of old friends one can <span role="region" aria-label="Referenced Content"><u>endure</u></span> with equanimity. But even a momentary separation from anyone to whom one has just been introduced is almost unbearable.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>As used in the text, what does the word &ldquo;endure&rdquo; most nearly mean?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"3b2330fa-616d-4808-a156-ee2f73ec344c","content":"<p>Regret</p>"},{"id":"40c9fffd-684e-49d8-bda2-ba5d62b18489","content":"<p>Persist</p>"},{"id":"491fe23a-05cd-4a7e-b9e1-21f99a987f76","content":"<p>Tolerate</p>"},{"id":"b2c96502-fc7e-485e-8060-c323eb29bc5c","content":"<p>Encourage</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["491fe23a-05cd-4a7e-b9e1-21f99a987f76"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer because as used in the text, &ldquo;endure&rdquo; most nearly means tolerate. In the text, Cecily and Algernon discuss parting, or saying goodbye. Cecily remarks on the deep pain of saying goodbye to people whom one has only known briefly and then comments on the equanimity, or calm steadiness, one experiences when separated from old friends. The text sets up an ironic contrast: one can easily tolerate, or put up with, the absence of close friends, but even a very short separation from a new acquaintance is unbearable. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. Nothing in the text suggests that Cecily associates regret, or a feeling of sorrow, with the absence of old friends. Rather, the text sets up an ironic contrast between the feeling of calm steadiness one feels when separated from old friends and the unbearable pain of being separated from new acquaintances. Choice B is incorrect. Although in some contexts &ldquo;endure&rdquo; can mean persist, or proceed stubbornly, it doesn&rsquo;t have that meaning in this context because what is being endured is the absence of old friends. Whereas one can persist <em>despite</em> the absence of someone else, one can&rsquo;t persist the absence itself. Choice D is incorrect because the text doesn&rsquo;t convey that Cecily encourages, or urges, old friends to be absent. Although it may be that Cecily prefers new acquaintances to friends she has known for a long time, the text focuses on her feelings as a result of others&rsquo; absences, not on her treatment of others. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-031-WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$336dbfdb-44d3-40b4-9a13-68156ed3dcd5$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"3566120b","external_id":"9d21eb26-86d3-4193-9e31-607f67f13f4e","vaultid":"052c1e78-31ab-40e0-a83b-45174447078d","uId":"85ebf7af-675c-4cad-8c80-8ef4a4e7beed","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"WIC","skill_desc":"Words in Context","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":6,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>The following text is adapted from Oscar Wilde&rsquo;s 1895 play <em>The Importance of Being Earnest</em>.</p>\n<p style=\"padding-left:1em\">CECILY: Have we got to part?</p>\n<p style=\"padding-left:1em\">ALGERNON: I am afraid so. It&rsquo;s a very painful parting.</p>\n<p style=\"padding-left:1em\"s>CECILY: It is always painful to part from people whom one has known for a very brief space of time. The absence of old friends one can <span role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Referenced Content\"><u>endure</u></span> with equanimity. But even a momentary separation from anyone to whom one has just been introduced is almost unbearable.</p>","stem":"<p>As used in the text, what does the word &ldquo;endure&rdquo; most nearly mean?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"3b2330fa-616d-4808-a156-ee2f73ec344c","content":"<p>Regret</p>"},{"id":"40c9fffd-684e-49d8-bda2-ba5d62b18489","content":"<p>Persist</p>"},{"id":"491fe23a-05cd-4a7e-b9e1-21f99a987f76","content":"<p>Tolerate</p>"},{"id":"b2c96502-fc7e-485e-8060-c323eb29bc5c","content":"<p>Encourage</p>"}],"keys":["491fe23a-05cd-4a7e-b9e1-21f99a987f76"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer because as used in the text, &ldquo;endure&rdquo; most nearly means tolerate. In the text, Cecily and Algernon discuss parting, or saying goodbye. Cecily remarks on the deep pain of saying goodbye to people whom one has only known briefly and then comments on the equanimity, or calm steadiness, one experiences when separated from old friends. The text sets up an ironic contrast: one can easily tolerate, or put up with, the absence of close friends, but even a very short separation from a new acquaintance is unbearable. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. Nothing in the text suggests that Cecily associates regret, or a feeling of sorrow, with the absence of old friends. Rather, the text sets up an ironic contrast between the feeling of calm steadiness one feels when separated from old friends and the unbearable pain of being separated from new acquaintances. Choice B is incorrect. Although in some contexts &ldquo;endure&rdquo; can mean persist, or proceed stubbornly, it doesn&rsquo;t have that meaning in this context because what is being endured is the absence of old friends. Whereas one can persist <em>despite</em> the absence of someone else, one can&rsquo;t persist the absence itself. Choice D is incorrect because the text doesn&rsquo;t convey that Cecily encourages, or urges, old friends to be absent. Although it may be that Cecily prefers new acquaintances to friends she has known for a long time, the text focuses on her feelings as a result of others&rsquo; absences, not on her treatment of others. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-031-WIC","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"336dbfdb-44d3-40b4-9a13-68156ed3dcd5","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959319,"updateDate":1691007959319}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959319, 1691007959319),
    ($SATQ$359902ae$SATQ$, $SATQ$2fcaff3f-b91a-493d-b21c-94e107bcf668$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$6c2bc8db-4e2e-4f9e-920e-7a7cd5bb18bd$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Words in Context$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The following text is adapted from Nathaniel Hawthorne&rsquo;s 1837 story &ldquo;Dr. Heidegger&rsquo;s Experiment.&rdquo; The main character, a physician, is experimenting with rehydrating a dried flower.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>At first [the rose] lay lightly on the surface of the fluid, appearing to imbibe none of its moisture. Soon, however, <span role="region" aria-label="Referenced Content"><u>a singular</u></span> change began to be visible. The crushed and dried petals stirred and assumed a deepening tinge of crimson, as if the flower were reviving from a deathlike slumber.</p>
</blockquote>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>As used in the text, what does the phrase &ldquo;a singular&rdquo; most nearly mean?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"47ab8092-48f1-497b-a85e-ae423d308f76","content":"<p>A lonely</p>"},{"id":"932357ca-6aff-48e2-ab1a-5c18f8955881","content":"<p>A disagreeable</p>"},{"id":"144d5030-833d-46f9-aea9-d02e1be81e8e","content":"<p>An acceptable</p>"},{"id":"1f93872d-662c-4a7c-aa83-381dc5f497d3","content":"<p>An extraordinary</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["1f93872d-662c-4a7c-aa83-381dc5f497d3"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer because as used in the text, &ldquo;singular&rdquo; most nearly means extraordinary. The text portrays an experiment in which a character rehydrates a dried rose by infusing it with moisture. After prolonged contact with the liquid, the rose begins to absorb it, undergoing an exceptional transformation: its color deepens, its previously &ldquo;crushed and dried&rdquo; petals shift, and the entire flower revives &ldquo;from a deathlike slumber.&rdquo; In other words, an extraordinary change is visible in the flower. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. Although in some contexts &ldquo;singular&rdquo; can mean of or relating to an individual or to a single instance of something, this usage doesn&rsquo;t imply loneliness or an otherwise unsatisfactory condition of isolation. Moreover, the text doesn&rsquo;t attribute such a condition to the rose. Choice B is incorrect. Although &ldquo;singular&rdquo; has several related meanings, none of them relate to being disagreeable or unpleasant. Moreover, the text doesn&rsquo;t portray the change undergone by the rose as necessarily disagreeable. Choice C is incorrect because &ldquo;singular&rdquo; means extraordinary, not acceptable. The change is portrayed as striking, not barely satisfactory. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-KA-WIC-M001$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$d39f991a-9c5e-4f18-bc31-457bd235e5a2$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"359902ae","external_id":"2fcaff3f-b91a-493d-b21c-94e107bcf668","vaultid":"6c2bc8db-4e2e-4f9e-920e-7a7cd5bb18bd","uId":"00f4916f-6514-4d16-a999-52452f0b4428","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"WIC","skill_desc":"Words in Context","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>The following text is adapted from Nathaniel Hawthorne&rsquo;s 1837 story &ldquo;Dr. Heidegger&rsquo;s Experiment.&rdquo; The main character, a physician, is experimenting with rehydrating a dried flower.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>At first [the rose] lay lightly on the surface of the fluid, appearing to imbibe none of its moisture. Soon, however, <span role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Referenced Content\"><u>a singular</u></span> change began to be visible. The crushed and dried petals stirred and assumed a deepening tinge of crimson, as if the flower were reviving from a deathlike slumber.</p>\n</blockquote>","stem":"<p>As used in the text, what does the phrase &ldquo;a singular&rdquo; most nearly mean?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"47ab8092-48f1-497b-a85e-ae423d308f76","content":"<p>A lonely</p>"},{"id":"932357ca-6aff-48e2-ab1a-5c18f8955881","content":"<p>A disagreeable</p>"},{"id":"144d5030-833d-46f9-aea9-d02e1be81e8e","content":"<p>An acceptable</p>"},{"id":"1f93872d-662c-4a7c-aa83-381dc5f497d3","content":"<p>An extraordinary</p>"}],"keys":["1f93872d-662c-4a7c-aa83-381dc5f497d3"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer because as used in the text, &ldquo;singular&rdquo; most nearly means extraordinary. The text portrays an experiment in which a character rehydrates a dried rose by infusing it with moisture. After prolonged contact with the liquid, the rose begins to absorb it, undergoing an exceptional transformation: its color deepens, its previously &ldquo;crushed and dried&rdquo; petals shift, and the entire flower revives &ldquo;from a deathlike slumber.&rdquo; In other words, an extraordinary change is visible in the flower. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. Although in some contexts &ldquo;singular&rdquo; can mean of or relating to an individual or to a single instance of something, this usage doesn&rsquo;t imply loneliness or an otherwise unsatisfactory condition of isolation. Moreover, the text doesn&rsquo;t attribute such a condition to the rose. Choice B is incorrect. Although &ldquo;singular&rdquo; has several related meanings, none of them relate to being disagreeable or unpleasant. Moreover, the text doesn&rsquo;t portray the change undergone by the rose as necessarily disagreeable. Choice C is incorrect because &ldquo;singular&rdquo; means extraordinary, not acceptable. The change is portrayed as striking, not barely satisfactory. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-KA-WIC-M001","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"d39f991a-9c5e-4f18-bc31-457bd235e5a2","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959378,"updateDate":1691007959378}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959378, 1691007959378),
    ($SATQ$359b891c$SATQ$, $SATQ$ed64f415-992e-4d5f-b6d6-b247388e46db$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Words in Context$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Painter Alma <span data-ssml-sub-alias="W">W.</span> Thomas was fascinated by the colors and shapes found in nature. The flowers and trees in the garden at her home in Washington, DC, <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> her work. For example, Thomas&rsquo;s use of broken brushstrokes was inspired by the way that light would shine through the leaves of a tree in front of her house.&nbsp;</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"7258ecdb-ff86-415c-a9ce-a5255eac7130","content":"<p>restricted</p>"},{"id":"9d74b01c-301c-4f9a-bb7d-0b7810dddbdd","content":"<p>announced</p>"},{"id":"0420c3c5-d1af-439a-a5fd-0f1c1b957bc4","content":"<p>distracted</p>"},{"id":"c618cad0-0cb9-4763-9400-c5c111d73df5","content":"<p>influenced</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["c618cad0-0cb9-4763-9400-c5c111d73df5"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of painter Alma <span data-ssml-sub-alias="W">W.</span> Thomas&rsquo;s work. In this context, "influenced" means to have had an effect on something&rsquo;s development. The text indicates that there is a connection between Thomas&rsquo;s work and the flowers and trees in her home&rsquo;s garden, giving the example of Thomas&rsquo;s brushstrokes being inspired by light shining through the leaves of a tree in front of her house. This context conveys that Thomas&rsquo;s work was influenced by the flowers and trees in the garden.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the text conveys that Thomas drew inspiration for her work from the plants in her garden, which suggests that the flowers and trees contributed positively to her work, not that they "restricted," or limited, her work. Choice B is incorrect because it wouldn&rsquo;t make sense to suggest that flowers and trees in a garden could have "announced," or made known, a painter&rsquo;s work. Choice C is incorrect because the text conveys that Thomas drew inspiration for her work from the plants in her garden, which suggests that the flowers and trees contributed positively to her work, not that they were a distraction. Further, it&rsquo;s not clear how an artist&rsquo;s work could itself be "distracted."</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"359b891c","external_id":"ed64f415-992e-4d5f-b6d6-b247388e46db","vaultid":null,"uId":"dbf61d38-b59b-4ee1-8587-5d9d4a2a8848","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"WIC","skill_desc":"Words in Context","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":1,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Painter Alma <span data-ssml-sub-alias=\"W\">W.</span> Thomas was fascinated by the colors and shapes found in nature. The flowers and trees in the garden at her home in Washington, DC, <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> her work. For example, Thomas&rsquo;s use of broken brushstrokes was inspired by the way that light would shine through the leaves of a tree in front of her house.&nbsp;</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"7258ecdb-ff86-415c-a9ce-a5255eac7130","content":"<p>restricted</p>"},{"id":"9d74b01c-301c-4f9a-bb7d-0b7810dddbdd","content":"<p>announced</p>"},{"id":"0420c3c5-d1af-439a-a5fd-0f1c1b957bc4","content":"<p>distracted</p>"},{"id":"c618cad0-0cb9-4763-9400-c5c111d73df5","content":"<p>influenced</p>"}],"keys":["c618cad0-0cb9-4763-9400-c5c111d73df5"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of painter Alma <span data-ssml-sub-alias=\"W\">W.</span> Thomas&rsquo;s work. In this context, \"influenced\" means to have had an effect on something&rsquo;s development. The text indicates that there is a connection between Thomas&rsquo;s work and the flowers and trees in her home&rsquo;s garden, giving the example of Thomas&rsquo;s brushstrokes being inspired by light shining through the leaves of a tree in front of her house. This context conveys that Thomas&rsquo;s work was influenced by the flowers and trees in the garden.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the text conveys that Thomas drew inspiration for her work from the plants in her garden, which suggests that the flowers and trees contributed positively to her work, not that they \"restricted,\" or limited, her work. Choice B is incorrect because it wouldn&rsquo;t make sense to suggest that flowers and trees in a garden could have \"announced,\" or made known, a painter&rsquo;s work. Choice C is incorrect because the text conveys that Thomas drew inspiration for her work from the plants in her garden, which suggests that the flowers and trees contributed positively to her work, not that they were a distraction. Further, it&rsquo;s not clear how an artist&rsquo;s work could itself be \"distracted.\"</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1743430669215,"updateDate":1743430669215}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1743430669215, 1743430669215),
    ($SATQ$35e21b06$SATQ$, $SATQ$afd5820f-5c20-4181-a439-a86c8a980a9c$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$82062bae-c2b0-4e56-9d4d-13658ab0d434$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$CTC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Cross-Text Connections$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 6, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p><span role="heading" aria-level="2"><strong>Text 1</strong></span></p>
<p>Dominique Potvin and colleagues captured five Australian magpies (<em>Gymnorhina tibicen</em>) to test a new design for attaching tracking devices to birds. As the researchers fitted each magpie with a tracker attached by a small harness, they noticed some magpies without trackers pecking at another magpie&rsquo;s tracker until it broke off. The researchers suggest that this behavior could be evidence of magpies attempting to help another magpie without benefiting themselves.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><span role="heading" aria-level="2"><strong>Text 2</strong></span></p>
<p>It can be tempting to think that animals are deliberately providing help when we see them removing trackers and other equipment from one another, especially when a species is known to exhibit other cooperative behaviors. At the same time, it can be difficult to exclude the possibility that individuals are simply interested in the equipment because of its novelty, curiously pawing or pecking at it until it detaches.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the researchers&rsquo; perspective in Text 1 on the behavior of the magpies without trackers?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"89bef27e-2ff7-411f-ac72-38d235ee90cd","content":"<p>That behavior might have been due to the novelty of the magpies&rsquo; captive setting rather than to the novelty of the tracker.</p>"},{"id":"45bf9ff2-e441-48d2-b998-ce499c509dd3","content":"<p>That behavior likely indicates that the magpies were deliberately attempting to benefit themselves by obtaining the tracker.</p>"},{"id":"253b4461-8065-4605-837b-96d3c9fba2f0","content":"<p>That behavior may not be evidence of selflessness in <em>Gymnorhina tibicen</em> because not all the captured magpies demonstrated it.</p>"},{"id":"c098f111-6216-40c9-8b1b-eca89b910249","content":"<p>That behavior might be adequately explained without suggesting that the magpies were attempting to assist the other magpie.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["c098f111-6216-40c9-8b1b-eca89b910249"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer because it reflects how the author of Text 2 would most likely respond to the researchers&rsquo; perspective in Text 1 on the behavior of the magpies without trackers. According to Text 1, Dominique Potvin and colleagues observed magpies without trackers pecking at a tracker on another magpie until the device fell off. The researchers suggested that the birds might have been attempting to help the other bird, with no benefit to themselves. Text 2 generally discusses scenarios in which animals have been observed removing trackers from each other. The text cautions that it shouldn&rsquo;t be assumed that these animals are helping one another deliberately, since they might simply be pecking at trackers out of curiosity, causing them to fall off eventually. Therefore, the author of Text 2 would most likely respond to Potvin and colleagues&rsquo; perspective in Text 1 by saying that the behavior of the magpies without trackers could be adequately explained without suggesting that they were attempting to assist the other magpie.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because Text 2 never discusses the novelty, or the newness and unusual quality, of the captive settings in which animals have been observed to remove trackers from other animals, nor does it suggest that such novelty might account for this behavior. Instead, the text suggests that it&rsquo;s the novelty of the tracking equipment itself that might cause the behavior: interested in the trackers because they&rsquo;re unusual, animals might paw or peck at them until they fall off. Choice B is incorrect because Text 2 never suggests that when animals remove trackers from other animals, they do so because they wish to obtain the trackers for themselves. Instead, Text 2 argues that animals paw or peck at trackers because they are merely curious about them. Choice C is incorrect because Text 2 doesn&rsquo;t argue that when captured animals are observed removing trackers from each other, their behavior should be regarded as selfless only if all of them participate in it. Instead, the text argues that the behavior may not be selfless at all and may instead be attributed to animals&rsquo; curiosity about the new and unusual trackers.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-015-CTC$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$14b5ce6f-db24-487a-bbbd-b44470324126$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"35e21b06","external_id":"afd5820f-5c20-4181-a439-a86c8a980a9c","vaultid":"82062bae-c2b0-4e56-9d4d-13658ab0d434","uId":"fad59b47-149c-48cb-8eb2-2b280276ab82","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"CTC","skill_desc":"Cross-Text Connections","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":6,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p><span role=\"heading\" aria-level=\"2\"><strong>Text 1</strong></span></p>\n<p>Dominique Potvin and colleagues captured five Australian magpies (<em>Gymnorhina tibicen</em>) to test a new design for attaching tracking devices to birds. As the researchers fitted each magpie with a tracker attached by a small harness, they noticed some magpies without trackers pecking at another magpie&rsquo;s tracker until it broke off. The researchers suggest that this behavior could be evidence of magpies attempting to help another magpie without benefiting themselves.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p><span role=\"heading\" aria-level=\"2\"><strong>Text 2</strong></span></p>\n<p>It can be tempting to think that animals are deliberately providing help when we see them removing trackers and other equipment from one another, especially when a species is known to exhibit other cooperative behaviors. At the same time, it can be difficult to exclude the possibility that individuals are simply interested in the equipment because of its novelty, curiously pawing or pecking at it until it detaches.</p>","stem":"<p>Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the researchers&rsquo; perspective in Text 1 on the behavior of the magpies without trackers?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"89bef27e-2ff7-411f-ac72-38d235ee90cd","content":"<p>That behavior might have been due to the novelty of the magpies&rsquo; captive setting rather than to the novelty of the tracker.</p>"},{"id":"45bf9ff2-e441-48d2-b998-ce499c509dd3","content":"<p>That behavior likely indicates that the magpies were deliberately attempting to benefit themselves by obtaining the tracker.</p>"},{"id":"253b4461-8065-4605-837b-96d3c9fba2f0","content":"<p>That behavior may not be evidence of selflessness in <em>Gymnorhina tibicen</em> because not all the captured magpies demonstrated it.</p>"},{"id":"c098f111-6216-40c9-8b1b-eca89b910249","content":"<p>That behavior might be adequately explained without suggesting that the magpies were attempting to assist the other magpie.</p>"}],"keys":["c098f111-6216-40c9-8b1b-eca89b910249"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer because it reflects how the author of Text 2 would most likely respond to the researchers&rsquo; perspective in Text 1 on the behavior of the magpies without trackers. According to Text 1, Dominique Potvin and colleagues observed magpies without trackers pecking at a tracker on another magpie until the device fell off. The researchers suggested that the birds might have been attempting to help the other bird, with no benefit to themselves. Text 2 generally discusses scenarios in which animals have been observed removing trackers from each other. The text cautions that it shouldn&rsquo;t be assumed that these animals are helping one another deliberately, since they might simply be pecking at trackers out of curiosity, causing them to fall off eventually. Therefore, the author of Text 2 would most likely respond to Potvin and colleagues&rsquo; perspective in Text 1 by saying that the behavior of the magpies without trackers could be adequately explained without suggesting that they were attempting to assist the other magpie.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because Text 2 never discusses the novelty, or the newness and unusual quality, of the captive settings in which animals have been observed to remove trackers from other animals, nor does it suggest that such novelty might account for this behavior. Instead, the text suggests that it&rsquo;s the novelty of the tracking equipment itself that might cause the behavior: interested in the trackers because they&rsquo;re unusual, animals might paw or peck at them until they fall off. Choice B is incorrect because Text 2 never suggests that when animals remove trackers from other animals, they do so because they wish to obtain the trackers for themselves. Instead, Text 2 argues that animals paw or peck at trackers because they are merely curious about them. Choice C is incorrect because Text 2 doesn&rsquo;t argue that when captured animals are observed removing trackers from each other, their behavior should be regarded as selfless only if all of them participate in it. Instead, the text argues that the behavior may not be selfless at all and may instead be attributed to animals&rsquo; curiosity about the new and unusual trackers.</p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-015-CTC","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"14b5ce6f-db24-487a-bbbd-b44470324126","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959315,"updateDate":1691007959315}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959315, 1691007959315),
    ($SATQ$35fd0eb4$SATQ$, $SATQ$f1366bb3-df7c-4ee0-8445-561b1496786a$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Words in Context$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The discoverers of the minor planet <span data-ssml-say-as="characters">1227</span> Geranium named it after the plant genus that includes cranesbills. Most of the recently discovered minor planets, however, are given only an identification number, largely due to there being over 500,000 such bodies known at present, which makes the already challenging task of finding a unique name for each nearly <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span>.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"317af447-a663-4c98-bbd8-e0d70b85b9d3","content":"<p>insurmountable</p>"},{"id":"b4a105ab-b87c-4bb0-bd3a-1355bf2e4894","content":"<p>feasible</p>"},{"id":"4d783554-00ab-4eb5-a368-d36b764bfe53","content":"<p>substantial</p>"},{"id":"64993a84-8b88-4b77-a203-fa992d306dba","content":"<p>unnecessary</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["317af447-a663-4c98-bbd8-e0d70b85b9d3"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of the difficulty in naming a large number of minor planets. In this context, "insurmountable" means impossible to overcome or too difficult to solve. The text establishes that coming up with distinct names for minor planets is already a difficult task that has become even more challenging because there are now more than 500,000 known minor planets. The logical conclusion is that finding unique names for such a vast number of celestial objects has become virtually impossible&mdash;or insurmountable&mdash;which explains why the majority of recently discovered minor planets receive only identification numbers instead of proper names.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect because "feasible" means possible to do or achieve, which would contradict the logic of the text. The text indicates that the task of naming minor planets has become more difficult because of their large number. The text then explains that this is why most new discoveries receive only identification numbers. If the task were feasible, there would be no reason to abandon the practice of giving proper names to minor planets in favor of using only numbers. Choice C is incorrect because "substantial" means considerable in size, amount, or importance. While the text does suggest that naming all minor planets would be a significant undertaking, describing the task as substantial wouldn&rsquo;t logically explain why the naming practice has been largely abandoned in favor of using only identification numbers. The text suggests that the task has become virtually impossible (insurmountable), not merely large or significant. Choice D is incorrect because "unnecessary," which means not needed, would suggest that giving proper names to minor planets serves no purpose. However, the text doesn&rsquo;t question the value or purpose of naming minor planets; rather, it explains that the sheer number of discoveries has made the already challenging naming task nearly impossible to complete. The text provides an example of a named minor planet (1227 Geranium), indicating that naming has been considered worthwhile but has become impractical because of the volume.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"35fd0eb4","external_id":"f1366bb3-df7c-4ee0-8445-561b1496786a","vaultid":null,"uId":"98ad06eb-aea4-4079-9d1f-46ba0dcffede","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"WIC","skill_desc":"Words in Context","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>The discoverers of the minor planet <span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">1227</span> Geranium named it after the plant genus that includes cranesbills. Most of the recently discovered minor planets, however, are given only an identification number, largely due to there being over 500,000 such bodies known at present, which makes the already challenging task of finding a unique name for each nearly <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span>.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"317af447-a663-4c98-bbd8-e0d70b85b9d3","content":"<p>insurmountable</p>"},{"id":"b4a105ab-b87c-4bb0-bd3a-1355bf2e4894","content":"<p>feasible</p>"},{"id":"4d783554-00ab-4eb5-a368-d36b764bfe53","content":"<p>substantial</p>"},{"id":"64993a84-8b88-4b77-a203-fa992d306dba","content":"<p>unnecessary</p>"}],"keys":["317af447-a663-4c98-bbd8-e0d70b85b9d3"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of the difficulty in naming a large number of minor planets. In this context, \"insurmountable\" means impossible to overcome or too difficult to solve. The text establishes that coming up with distinct names for minor planets is already a difficult task that has become even more challenging because there are now more than 500,000 known minor planets. The logical conclusion is that finding unique names for such a vast number of celestial objects has become virtually impossible&mdash;or insurmountable&mdash;which explains why the majority of recently discovered minor planets receive only identification numbers instead of proper names.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect because \"feasible\" means possible to do or achieve, which would contradict the logic of the text. The text indicates that the task of naming minor planets has become more difficult because of their large number. The text then explains that this is why most new discoveries receive only identification numbers. If the task were feasible, there would be no reason to abandon the practice of giving proper names to minor planets in favor of using only numbers. Choice C is incorrect because \"substantial\" means considerable in size, amount, or importance. While the text does suggest that naming all minor planets would be a significant undertaking, describing the task as substantial wouldn&rsquo;t logically explain why the naming practice has been largely abandoned in favor of using only identification numbers. The text suggests that the task has become virtually impossible (insurmountable), not merely large or significant. Choice D is incorrect because \"unnecessary,\" which means not needed, would suggest that giving proper names to minor planets serves no purpose. However, the text doesn&rsquo;t question the value or purpose of naming minor planets; rather, it explains that the sheer number of discoveries has made the already challenging naming task nearly impossible to complete. The text provides an example of a named minor planet (1227 Geranium), indicating that naming has been considered worthwhile but has become impractical because of the volume.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1755117061192,"updateDate":1755117061192}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1755117061192, 1755117061192),
    ($SATQ$3654202f$SATQ$, $SATQ$f0b1f9fc-75aa-41dd-8a8c-c7686e6629b6$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Words in Context$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 3, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Radar and sonar can detect objects or people through walls, but they are too costly for consumer use. Researchers Fadel Adib and Dina Katabi have shown, however, that there may be a comparatively <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> alternative: small changes to the Wi-Fi technology used in smartphones can give users a cheap way to count people or even identify their gestures through walls.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"34783b63-3e5d-4170-8e17-4e04ca9eb635","content":"<p>infrequent</p>"},{"id":"ad706728-e742-4995-8793-af6f759d0a19","content":"<p>inexpensive</p>"},{"id":"d3002e3e-9580-4647-bd51-fe680c3ed038","content":"<p>unmistakable</p>"},{"id":"5a573417-f90b-4a72-bcec-dfc17ee696c7","content":"<p>impractical</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["ad706728-e742-4995-8793-af6f759d0a19"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of ways to detect people through walls. In this context, "inexpensive" means less costly or low in price. The passage describes two kinds of methods that can be used to "detect objects or people through walls": radar and sonar, and changes to the Wi-Fi technology used in smartphones. The word "comparatively" before the blank indicates that the best answer would be one that helps to describe a comparison between the two. Radar and sonar are described as "costly," while the method that uses Wi-Fi technology is described as "cheap"; therefore, it would be comparatively affordable, or inexpensive.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the text indicates that a small modification to the smartphones that many consumers already own would presumably result in consumers using that technology to detect objects or people through walls more regularly, or frequently; therefore, it would not be true that such technology would be comparatively infrequent, or uncommon. Choice C is incorrect because there is nothing in the text to indicate that either radar and sonar or changes to smartphone Wi-Fi technology are capable of being misunderstood or mistaken for something else; therefore, it would not make sense to describe changes to Wi-Fi smartphone technology as comparatively unmistakable, or indisputable. Choice D is incorrect because the text indicates that a small modification to the smartphones that many consumers already own and carry with them would be more functional or practical than radar and sonar, not more impractical, or unrealistic.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"3654202f","external_id":"f0b1f9fc-75aa-41dd-8a8c-c7686e6629b6","vaultid":null,"uId":"68bc06e2-66e5-41e5-873a-d2f32f84901c","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"WIC","skill_desc":"Words in Context","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":3,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Radar and sonar can detect objects or people through walls, but they are too costly for consumer use. Researchers Fadel Adib and Dina Katabi have shown, however, that there may be a comparatively <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> alternative: small changes to the Wi-Fi technology used in smartphones can give users a cheap way to count people or even identify their gestures through walls.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"34783b63-3e5d-4170-8e17-4e04ca9eb635","content":"<p>infrequent</p>"},{"id":"ad706728-e742-4995-8793-af6f759d0a19","content":"<p>inexpensive</p>"},{"id":"d3002e3e-9580-4647-bd51-fe680c3ed038","content":"<p>unmistakable</p>"},{"id":"5a573417-f90b-4a72-bcec-dfc17ee696c7","content":"<p>impractical</p>"}],"keys":["ad706728-e742-4995-8793-af6f759d0a19"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of ways to detect people through walls. In this context, \"inexpensive\" means less costly or low in price. The passage describes two kinds of methods that can be used to \"detect objects or people through walls\": radar and sonar, and changes to the Wi-Fi technology used in smartphones. The word \"comparatively\" before the blank indicates that the best answer would be one that helps to describe a comparison between the two. Radar and sonar are described as \"costly,\" while the method that uses Wi-Fi technology is described as \"cheap\"; therefore, it would be comparatively affordable, or inexpensive.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the text indicates that a small modification to the smartphones that many consumers already own would presumably result in consumers using that technology to detect objects or people through walls more regularly, or frequently; therefore, it would not be true that such technology would be comparatively infrequent, or uncommon. Choice C is incorrect because there is nothing in the text to indicate that either radar and sonar or changes to smartphone Wi-Fi technology are capable of being misunderstood or mistaken for something else; therefore, it would not make sense to describe changes to Wi-Fi smartphone technology as comparatively unmistakable, or indisputable. Choice D is incorrect because the text indicates that a small modification to the smartphones that many consumers already own and carry with them would be more functional or practical than radar and sonar, not more impractical, or unrealistic.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323033,"updateDate":1730147323033}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323033, 1730147323033),
    ($SATQ$37219ba2$SATQ$, $SATQ$4afa64a5-d8ae-4ef9-bdbd-2966534f1180$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Words in Context$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The following text is from John Muir&rsquo;s 1913 autobiography <em>The Story of My Boyhood and Youth</em>. Muir describes being on a boat.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The water was so <span style="text-decoration: underline;" role="region" aria-label="Referenced Content">clear</span> that it was almost invisible, and when we floated slowly out over the plants and fishes, we seemed to be miraculously sustained in the air while exploring a veritable fairyland.</p>
</blockquote>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>As used in the text, what does the word &ldquo;clear&rdquo; most nearly mean?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"7bee3235-8ebf-41f3-9c33-281f84dd719b","content":"<p>Simple</p>"},{"id":"b000260e-a00e-4dd6-a89b-60e2cf90760e","content":"<p>Understandable</p>"},{"id":"55c7b02c-15b1-40bd-98ac-e4f4afd7ec22","content":"<p>Obvious</p>"},{"id":"e1ed6ed0-729c-4b3e-83d0-2f8b36476f3e","content":"<p>Transparent</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["e1ed6ed0-729c-4b3e-83d0-2f8b36476f3e"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer because as used in the text, "clear" most nearly means transparent, or see-through. Muir states that the water beneath the boat "was so clear that it was almost invisible," suggesting that those on the boat were able to see through the water and easily observe plants and fish below the surface.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. In some contexts "clear" can mean "simple," or uncomplicated, but Muir is describing the water, and water isn&rsquo;t typically described as either simple or complicated. Muir emphasizes the water&rsquo;s transparency, not its simplicity. Choice B is incorrect. In some contexts "clear" can mean "understandable," or reasonable or easily comprehended, but Muir is describing the water, and it doesn&rsquo;t make much sense to describe water as understandable. Muir emphasizes the water&rsquo;s transparency, not how easily the water can be understood. Choice C is incorrect. Although "clear" can mean "obvious," or easily seen or understood, in some contexts, Muir&rsquo;s description emphasizes that the water "was almost invisible" and that the boat seemed to be "sustained in the air," suggesting that the water was almost impossible to see, not that it was obvious.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"37219ba2","external_id":"4afa64a5-d8ae-4ef9-bdbd-2966534f1180","vaultid":null,"uId":"38632d66-6710-46de-babe-f12db65f2d4c","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"WIC","skill_desc":"Words in Context","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":2,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>The following text is from John Muir&rsquo;s 1913 autobiography <em>The Story of My Boyhood and Youth</em>. Muir describes being on a boat.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>The water was so <span style=\"text-decoration: underline;\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Referenced Content\">clear</span> that it was almost invisible, and when we floated slowly out over the plants and fishes, we seemed to be miraculously sustained in the air while exploring a veritable fairyland.</p>\n</blockquote>","stem":"<p>As used in the text, what does the word &ldquo;clear&rdquo; most nearly mean?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"7bee3235-8ebf-41f3-9c33-281f84dd719b","content":"<p>Simple</p>"},{"id":"b000260e-a00e-4dd6-a89b-60e2cf90760e","content":"<p>Understandable</p>"},{"id":"55c7b02c-15b1-40bd-98ac-e4f4afd7ec22","content":"<p>Obvious</p>"},{"id":"e1ed6ed0-729c-4b3e-83d0-2f8b36476f3e","content":"<p>Transparent</p>"}],"keys":["e1ed6ed0-729c-4b3e-83d0-2f8b36476f3e"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer because as used in the text, \"clear\" most nearly means transparent, or see-through. Muir states that the water beneath the boat \"was so clear that it was almost invisible,\" suggesting that those on the boat were able to see through the water and easily observe plants and fish below the surface.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. In some contexts \"clear\" can mean \"simple,\" or uncomplicated, but Muir is describing the water, and water isn&rsquo;t typically described as either simple or complicated. Muir emphasizes the water&rsquo;s transparency, not its simplicity. Choice B is incorrect. In some contexts \"clear\" can mean \"understandable,\" or reasonable or easily comprehended, but Muir is describing the water, and it doesn&rsquo;t make much sense to describe water as understandable. Muir emphasizes the water&rsquo;s transparency, not how easily the water can be understood. Choice C is incorrect. Although \"clear\" can mean \"obvious,\" or easily seen or understood, in some contexts, Muir&rsquo;s description emphasizes that the water \"was almost invisible\" and that the boat seemed to be \"sustained in the air,\" suggesting that the water was almost impossible to see, not that it was obvious.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1743430669206,"updateDate":1743430669206}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1743430669206, 1743430669206),
    ($SATQ$39857700$SATQ$, $SATQ$db577fba-f840-47e8-adf4-84d7e79e8c3e$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$a10b638a-8d68-401d-b034-f95888da115c$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$TSP$SATQ$, $SATQ$Text Structure and Purpose$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 6, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The following text is from Edith Wharton&rsquo;s 1905 novel <em>The House of Mirth</em>. Lily Bart and a companion are walking through a park.</p>
<p style='padding-left: 1em;'>Lily had no real intimacy with nature, but she had a passion for the appropriate and could be keenly sensitive to a scene which was the fitting background of her own sensations. <span role="region" aria-label="Referenced Content"><u>The landscape outspread below her seemed an enlargement of her present mood, and she found something of herself in its calmness, its breadth, its long free reaches.</u></span> On the nearer slopes the sugar-maples wavered like pyres of light; lower down was a massing of grey orchards, and here and there the lingering green of an oak-grove.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"bbcb4318-71f8-40c0-9c53-2b0b807f37e3","content":"<p>It creates a detailed image of the physical setting of the scene.</p>"},{"id":"ada4602d-0e18-442b-924f-1635ccc9676f","content":"<p>It establishes that a character is experiencing an internal conflict.</p>"},{"id":"4c7131d0-6a00-4207-88ea-15ccc54e4ea9","content":"<p>It makes an assertion that the next sentence then expands on.</p>"},{"id":"8f6f9acd-3864-422c-b318-5128508f6076","content":"<p>It illustrates an idea that is introduced in the previous sentence.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["8f6f9acd-3864-422c-b318-5128508f6076"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer because it best describes how the underlined sentence functions in the text as a whole. The first sentence of the text establishes that Lily can be &ldquo;keenly sensitive to&rdquo; scenes that serve as a &ldquo;fitting background&rdquo; for her feelings&mdash;that is, she&rsquo;s very aware of when a setting seems to reflect her mood. The next sentence, which is underlined, then demonstrates this awareness: Lily views the landscape she&rsquo;s in as a large-scale reflection of her current mood, identifying with elements such as its calmness. Thus, the function of the underlined sentence is to illustrate an idea introduced in the previous sentence. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the underlined sentence describes the scene only in very general terms, referring to its calmness, breadth, and long stretches of land. It&rsquo;s the next sentence that adds specific details about colors, light, and various trees nearby. Choice B is incorrect because nothing in the underlined sentence suggests that Lily is experiencing an internal conflict. In fact, the sentence indicates that Lily thinks the landscape reflects her own feeling of calmness. Choice C is incorrect because the only assertion in the underlined sentence is that Lily feels that broad aspects of the landscape, such as its calmness, reflect her current mood, and that assertion isn&rsquo;t expanded on in the next sentence. Instead, the next sentence describes specific details of the scene without connecting them to Lily&rsquo;s feelings. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-018-TSP$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$962967a0-9967-4494-ac20-0b0191792957$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"39857700","external_id":"db577fba-f840-47e8-adf4-84d7e79e8c3e","vaultid":"a10b638a-8d68-401d-b034-f95888da115c","uId":"494abf7a-ebf2-4532-baa3-9a1a31df2d9f","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"TSP","skill_desc":"Text Structure and Purpose","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":6,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>The following text is from Edith Wharton&rsquo;s 1905 novel <em>The House of Mirth</em>. Lily Bart and a companion are walking through a park.</p>\n<p style='padding-left: 1em;'>Lily had no real intimacy with nature, but she had a passion for the appropriate and could be keenly sensitive to a scene which was the fitting background of her own sensations. <span role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Referenced Content\"><u>The landscape outspread below her seemed an enlargement of her present mood, and she found something of herself in its calmness, its breadth, its long free reaches.</u></span> On the nearer slopes the sugar-maples wavered like pyres of light; lower down was a massing of grey orchards, and here and there the lingering green of an oak-grove.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"bbcb4318-71f8-40c0-9c53-2b0b807f37e3","content":"<p>It creates a detailed image of the physical setting of the scene.</p>"},{"id":"ada4602d-0e18-442b-924f-1635ccc9676f","content":"<p>It establishes that a character is experiencing an internal conflict.</p>"},{"id":"4c7131d0-6a00-4207-88ea-15ccc54e4ea9","content":"<p>It makes an assertion that the next sentence then expands on.</p>"},{"id":"8f6f9acd-3864-422c-b318-5128508f6076","content":"<p>It illustrates an idea that is introduced in the previous sentence.</p>"}],"keys":["8f6f9acd-3864-422c-b318-5128508f6076"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer because it best describes how the underlined sentence functions in the text as a whole. The first sentence of the text establishes that Lily can be &ldquo;keenly sensitive to&rdquo; scenes that serve as a &ldquo;fitting background&rdquo; for her feelings&mdash;that is, she&rsquo;s very aware of when a setting seems to reflect her mood. The next sentence, which is underlined, then demonstrates this awareness: Lily views the landscape she&rsquo;s in as a large-scale reflection of her current mood, identifying with elements such as its calmness. Thus, the function of the underlined sentence is to illustrate an idea introduced in the previous sentence. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the underlined sentence describes the scene only in very general terms, referring to its calmness, breadth, and long stretches of land. It&rsquo;s the next sentence that adds specific details about colors, light, and various trees nearby. Choice B is incorrect because nothing in the underlined sentence suggests that Lily is experiencing an internal conflict. In fact, the sentence indicates that Lily thinks the landscape reflects her own feeling of calmness. Choice C is incorrect because the only assertion in the underlined sentence is that Lily feels that broad aspects of the landscape, such as its calmness, reflect her current mood, and that assertion isn&rsquo;t expanded on in the next sentence. Instead, the next sentence describes specific details of the scene without connecting them to Lily&rsquo;s feelings. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-018-TSP","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"962967a0-9967-4494-ac20-0b0191792957","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959316,"updateDate":1691007959316}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959316, 1691007959316),
    ($SATQ$3d658a5a$SATQ$, $SATQ$0fd533da-56e3-48d2-be14-a0a3becb6886$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$e66224ff-f3b9-4f42-ba59-f6472063cda3$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Words in Context$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 7, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Some foraging models predict that the distance bees travel when foraging will decline as floral density increases, but biologists Shalene Jha and Claire Kremen showed that bees&rsquo; behavior is inconsistent with this prediction if flowers in dense patches are <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span>: bees will forage beyond patches of low species richness to acquire multiple resource types.&nbsp;</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"5d58611f-7f0d-4c94-a115-b09c921e9316","content":"<p>depleted&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"b9036e67-fe93-4b19-859c-9ab55f9823ac","content":"<p>homogeneous</p>"},{"id":"49d0b50d-925f-418b-9f24-5cf5c49c50d9","content":"<p>immature</p>"},{"id":"aa899195-b099-4e87-bd4e-b0a0c2c094f4","content":"<p>dispersed</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["b9036e67-fe93-4b19-859c-9ab55f9823ac"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of Jha and Kremen&rsquo;s finding about bees&rsquo; foraging behavior. In this context, &ldquo;homogeneous&rdquo; means uniform or of the same kind. The text indicates that some models predict that the distance that bees travel when they&rsquo;re foraging declines as the density of flowers increases. The text goes on to say, however, that Jha and Kremen identified a circumstance in which bees don&rsquo;t behave this way. Specifically, if bees encounter &ldquo;patches of low species richness&rdquo;&mdash;that is, patches in which the flowers are largely from the same species&mdash;they&rsquo;ll travel beyond those patches to get varied food resources. This context thus suggests that bees don&rsquo;t behave as some models predict if the dense patches of flowers the bees encounter are homogeneous.&nbsp;</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the text indicates that Jha and Kremen found that bees will behave differently than some models predict if the bees encounter flower patches that are not rich in species, not if the flowers are &ldquo;depleted,&rdquo; or emptied or reduced in quality or quantity. Although it could be true that bees are likely to leave depleted patches in search of more resources, the text doesn&rsquo;t indicate that Jha and Kremen investigated that possibility. Choice C is incorrect because there&rsquo;s no information in the text suggesting that bees will not behave as some models predict if flowers in patches are &ldquo;immature,&rdquo; or not fully developed. Instead, the text indicates that Jha and Kremen found that bees will behave contrary to some models&rsquo; predictions if the flower patches are not rich in species. Choice D is incorrect because the text indicates that bees&rsquo; behavior will be inconsistent with the predictions of some models if the flower patches that the bees encounter are of low species richness, not if the flowers are in patches that are &ldquo;dispersed,&rdquo; or widely scattered. Although the text does describe bees as leaving patches that are not rich in species to forage elsewhere, there&rsquo;s no suggestion that Jha and Kremen found that the distance between dense flower patches affects whether the bees behave as some models predict. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-KA-WIC-H004$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$12305499-7cd3-4e2b-8263-f7199a8871cd$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"3d658a5a","external_id":"0fd533da-56e3-48d2-be14-a0a3becb6886","vaultid":"e66224ff-f3b9-4f42-ba59-f6472063cda3","uId":"bc6d6cd6-6de3-48da-a49b-6fad199ece8d","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"WIC","skill_desc":"Words in Context","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":7,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Some foraging models predict that the distance bees travel when foraging will decline as floral density increases, but biologists Shalene Jha and Claire Kremen showed that bees&rsquo; behavior is inconsistent with this prediction if flowers in dense patches are <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span>: bees will forage beyond patches of low species richness to acquire multiple resource types.&nbsp;</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"5d58611f-7f0d-4c94-a115-b09c921e9316","content":"<p>depleted&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"b9036e67-fe93-4b19-859c-9ab55f9823ac","content":"<p>homogeneous</p>"},{"id":"49d0b50d-925f-418b-9f24-5cf5c49c50d9","content":"<p>immature</p>"},{"id":"aa899195-b099-4e87-bd4e-b0a0c2c094f4","content":"<p>dispersed</p>"}],"keys":["b9036e67-fe93-4b19-859c-9ab55f9823ac"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of Jha and Kremen&rsquo;s finding about bees&rsquo; foraging behavior. In this context, &ldquo;homogeneous&rdquo; means uniform or of the same kind. The text indicates that some models predict that the distance that bees travel when they&rsquo;re foraging declines as the density of flowers increases. The text goes on to say, however, that Jha and Kremen identified a circumstance in which bees don&rsquo;t behave this way. Specifically, if bees encounter &ldquo;patches of low species richness&rdquo;&mdash;that is, patches in which the flowers are largely from the same species&mdash;they&rsquo;ll travel beyond those patches to get varied food resources. This context thus suggests that bees don&rsquo;t behave as some models predict if the dense patches of flowers the bees encounter are homogeneous.&nbsp;</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the text indicates that Jha and Kremen found that bees will behave differently than some models predict if the bees encounter flower patches that are not rich in species, not if the flowers are &ldquo;depleted,&rdquo; or emptied or reduced in quality or quantity. Although it could be true that bees are likely to leave depleted patches in search of more resources, the text doesn&rsquo;t indicate that Jha and Kremen investigated that possibility. Choice C is incorrect because there&rsquo;s no information in the text suggesting that bees will not behave as some models predict if flowers in patches are &ldquo;immature,&rdquo; or not fully developed. Instead, the text indicates that Jha and Kremen found that bees will behave contrary to some models&rsquo; predictions if the flower patches are not rich in species. Choice D is incorrect because the text indicates that bees&rsquo; behavior will be inconsistent with the predictions of some models if the flower patches that the bees encounter are of low species richness, not if the flowers are in patches that are &ldquo;dispersed,&rdquo; or widely scattered. Although the text does describe bees as leaving patches that are not rich in species to forage elsewhere, there&rsquo;s no suggestion that Jha and Kremen found that the distance between dense flower patches affects whether the bees behave as some models predict. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-KA-WIC-H004","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"12305499-7cd3-4e2b-8263-f7199a8871cd","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959377,"updateDate":1691007959377}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959377, 1691007959377),
    ($SATQ$3e63fce0$SATQ$, $SATQ$df2f8a80-cb38-4733-85ce-1de0f98ed846$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$TSP$SATQ$, $SATQ$Text Structure and Purpose$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>A team of researchers discovered that Matabele ants can identify an infected wound in a member of the colony and then treat the infection by covering the wound with antimicrobial secretions that the ants produce. The team found that the mortality rate for Matabele ants with infected injuries was reduced by 90% with this treatment, and they are hopeful that this discovery could aid in the development of new antibiotics for human use.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"80e4e141-c2f9-41ec-9706-4637b17e380c","content":"<p>It summarizes research findings on Matabele ants and then identifies an area for further research.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"86dad7c7-e4cd-42c9-aeb4-0797d3ee2340","content":"<p>It introduces a study of Matabele ants and then explains the research methods used in the study.</p>"},{"id":"72976f93-8d68-426f-9696-6bccc6fbfc3a","content":"<p>It describes unique properties of Matabele ants and then speculates on how those properties evolved.</p>"},{"id":"86bccda7-72d3-4b46-854c-dad92c1ae8b5","content":"<p>It identifies an issue concerning Matabele ants and then proposes a solution to address the issue.&nbsp;</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["80e4e141-c2f9-41ec-9706-4637b17e380c"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer because it most accurately describes the overall structure of the text. The text first presents what researchers found from studying Matabele ants: the ants can tell when colony members have infected wounds and can largely successfully treat those infections with their antimicrobial secretions. The text then indicates that the findings about the ants could help with the future development of antibiotics for humans. Thus, the text summarizes research findings and then identifies an area for further research.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect. Although the text introduces a study about Matabele ants, it never discusses how the researchers did their work or any methods they used to produce their findings. Choice C is incorrect. Although the text does describe properties of Matabele ants&mdash;their ability to detect and largely successfully treat infected wounds in colony members&mdash;it never addresses evolution or how these properties might have changed. Choice D is incorrect because the text doesn&rsquo;t present anything about Matabele ants as an issue or problem to be solved. Instead, it discusses beneficial abilities the ants have (detecting and treating infected wounds) and suggests that understanding those abilities might be useful for human medicine.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"3e63fce0","external_id":"df2f8a80-cb38-4733-85ce-1de0f98ed846","vaultid":null,"uId":"ed348862-4651-4895-b552-6bb65786bbd1","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"TSP","skill_desc":"Text Structure and Purpose","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>A team of researchers discovered that Matabele ants can identify an infected wound in a member of the colony and then treat the infection by covering the wound with antimicrobial secretions that the ants produce. The team found that the mortality rate for Matabele ants with infected injuries was reduced by 90% with this treatment, and they are hopeful that this discovery could aid in the development of new antibiotics for human use.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"80e4e141-c2f9-41ec-9706-4637b17e380c","content":"<p>It summarizes research findings on Matabele ants and then identifies an area for further research.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"86dad7c7-e4cd-42c9-aeb4-0797d3ee2340","content":"<p>It introduces a study of Matabele ants and then explains the research methods used in the study.</p>"},{"id":"72976f93-8d68-426f-9696-6bccc6fbfc3a","content":"<p>It describes unique properties of Matabele ants and then speculates on how those properties evolved.</p>"},{"id":"86bccda7-72d3-4b46-854c-dad92c1ae8b5","content":"<p>It identifies an issue concerning Matabele ants and then proposes a solution to address the issue.&nbsp;</p>"}],"keys":["80e4e141-c2f9-41ec-9706-4637b17e380c"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer because it most accurately describes the overall structure of the text. The text first presents what researchers found from studying Matabele ants: the ants can tell when colony members have infected wounds and can largely successfully treat those infections with their antimicrobial secretions. The text then indicates that the findings about the ants could help with the future development of antibiotics for humans. Thus, the text summarizes research findings and then identifies an area for further research.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect. Although the text introduces a study about Matabele ants, it never discusses how the researchers did their work or any methods they used to produce their findings. Choice C is incorrect. Although the text does describe properties of Matabele ants&mdash;their ability to detect and largely successfully treat infected wounds in colony members&mdash;it never addresses evolution or how these properties might have changed. Choice D is incorrect because the text doesn&rsquo;t present anything about Matabele ants as an issue or problem to be solved. Instead, it discusses beneficial abilities the ants have (detecting and treating infected wounds) and suggests that understanding those abilities might be useful for human medicine.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1776362771170,"updateDate":1776362771170}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1776362771170, 1776362771170),
    ($SATQ$3e6ad72d$SATQ$, $SATQ$27ab244a-eb2e-4ba2-a8c8-76ef13c5e2fb$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$8416dfb9-931f-49e9-ad35-07ec1b0ec6c5$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$TSP$SATQ$, $SATQ$Text Structure and Purpose$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 6, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>A study by a team including finance professor Madhu Veeraraghavan suggests that exposure to sunshine during the workday can lead to overly optimistic behavior. <span role="region" aria-label="Referenced Content"><u>Using data spanning from 1994 to 2010 for a set of US companies, the team compared over 29,000 annual earnings forecasts to the actual earnings later reported by those companies.</u></span> The team found that the greater the exposure to sunshine at work in the two weeks before a manager submitted an earnings forecast, the more the manager&rsquo;s forecast exceeded what the company actually earned that year.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice best states the function of the underlined sentence in the overall structure of the text?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"37fe9c63-64a6-46e1-bf28-a5e75c4d6bac","content":"<p>To summarize the results of the team&rsquo;s analysis</p>"},{"id":"d50015f7-b196-4a0a-a9cd-af459c056b35","content":"<p>To present a specific example that illustrates the study&rsquo;s findings</p>"},{"id":"5d06ba6b-4fad-4bd2-9272-9bab6f3449db","content":"<p>To explain part of the methodology used in the team&rsquo;s study</p>"},{"id":"2468c244-cfb0-4a9f-9e87-28557746f197","content":"<p>To call out a challenge the team faced in conducting its analysis</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["5d06ba6b-4fad-4bd2-9272-9bab6f3449db"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer because it best describes how the underlined sentence functions in the text as a whole. The first sentence presents the implications of Veeraraghavan&rsquo;s team&rsquo;s study: sunshine exposure during work hours can cause overly optimistic behavior. The underlined sentence then describes the data the team consulted and how they were used (comparing predictions about earnings to what the companies actually earned), and the final sentence presents what the team found in their examination of the data. Thus, the underlined sentence mainly functions to explain part of the methodology used in the team&rsquo;s study. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the underlined sentence explains in part how the team conducted their analysis of the effect of sunshine but doesn&rsquo;t address what the team found; a broad summary is instead given in the other two sentences. Choice B is incorrect because the underlined sentence doesn&rsquo;t present any specific examples from the team&rsquo;s comparisons of 29,000 earnings predictions to actual earnings; it simply explains in part how the team conducted their analysis.&nbsp;Choice D is incorrect because the underlined sentence simply explains in part how the team conducted their analysis; the text never mentions any challenges that the team encountered in their study. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-011-TSP$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$01a0c315-8350-4690-9c23-96dba6a58e9c$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"3e6ad72d","external_id":"27ab244a-eb2e-4ba2-a8c8-76ef13c5e2fb","vaultid":"8416dfb9-931f-49e9-ad35-07ec1b0ec6c5","uId":"5b1fdc2d-15c8-4a59-a9c1-283120066d2e","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"TSP","skill_desc":"Text Structure and Purpose","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":6,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>A study by a team including finance professor Madhu Veeraraghavan suggests that exposure to sunshine during the workday can lead to overly optimistic behavior. <span role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Referenced Content\"><u>Using data spanning from 1994 to 2010 for a set of US companies, the team compared over 29,000 annual earnings forecasts to the actual earnings later reported by those companies.</u></span> The team found that the greater the exposure to sunshine at work in the two weeks before a manager submitted an earnings forecast, the more the manager&rsquo;s forecast exceeded what the company actually earned that year.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice best states the function of the underlined sentence in the overall structure of the text?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"37fe9c63-64a6-46e1-bf28-a5e75c4d6bac","content":"<p>To summarize the results of the team&rsquo;s analysis</p>"},{"id":"d50015f7-b196-4a0a-a9cd-af459c056b35","content":"<p>To present a specific example that illustrates the study&rsquo;s findings</p>"},{"id":"5d06ba6b-4fad-4bd2-9272-9bab6f3449db","content":"<p>To explain part of the methodology used in the team&rsquo;s study</p>"},{"id":"2468c244-cfb0-4a9f-9e87-28557746f197","content":"<p>To call out a challenge the team faced in conducting its analysis</p>"}],"keys":["5d06ba6b-4fad-4bd2-9272-9bab6f3449db"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer because it best describes how the underlined sentence functions in the text as a whole. The first sentence presents the implications of Veeraraghavan&rsquo;s team&rsquo;s study: sunshine exposure during work hours can cause overly optimistic behavior. The underlined sentence then describes the data the team consulted and how they were used (comparing predictions about earnings to what the companies actually earned), and the final sentence presents what the team found in their examination of the data. Thus, the underlined sentence mainly functions to explain part of the methodology used in the team&rsquo;s study. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the underlined sentence explains in part how the team conducted their analysis of the effect of sunshine but doesn&rsquo;t address what the team found; a broad summary is instead given in the other two sentences. Choice B is incorrect because the underlined sentence doesn&rsquo;t present any specific examples from the team&rsquo;s comparisons of 29,000 earnings predictions to actual earnings; it simply explains in part how the team conducted their analysis.&nbsp;Choice D is incorrect because the underlined sentence simply explains in part how the team conducted their analysis; the text never mentions any challenges that the team encountered in their study. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-011-TSP","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"01a0c315-8350-4690-9c23-96dba6a58e9c","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959314,"updateDate":1691007959314}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959314, 1691007959314),
    ($SATQ$3ec184af$SATQ$, $SATQ$233c1b7d-48c1-434e-989f-84bcc9029899$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Words in Context$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Many ancient sculptures of people&rsquo;s heads are missing their noses. This is because the nose is the most <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> part of a sculpture of a person&rsquo;s head. It is delicate and sticks out from the rest of the sculpture, making it especially easy to break.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"36454b0b-2a06-49a8-8d8a-0c1acbb18cf5","content":"<p>recognizable</p>"},{"id":"7ab8bc1c-441a-4d5a-9fdf-22c6932aa039","content":"<p>fragile</p>"},{"id":"e1925cd8-98b2-4e59-82f6-e064cace2ab2","content":"<p>common</p>"},{"id":"3066a402-2f77-4c5c-bec1-c48f6e6d51db","content":"<p>sophisticated</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["7ab8bc1c-441a-4d5a-9fdf-22c6932aa039"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of noses on ancient sculptures. In this context, "fragile" means weak or delicate. This matches the text&rsquo;s description of noses on ancient sculptures, which are often missing from sculptures&rsquo; heads because they are "especially easy to break." Therefore, this context indicates that noses on ancient sculptures are the most fragile part of the sculptures&rsquo; heads.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. In this context, "recognizable" would mean identifiable, and since the text indicates that noses are often missing from ancient statues, they therefore cannot be the most recognizable part of the statue. Choice C is incorrect because the text indicates that many ancient statues are missing noses, so noses wouldn&rsquo;t be "common," or frequent, aspects of ancient statues; they would conversely be uncommon. Choice D is incorrect because the text only indicates that noses on ancient statues often stick out and end up missing from the heads, which doesn&rsquo;t relate to the noses being "sophisticated," or knowledgeable or refined.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"3ec184af","external_id":"233c1b7d-48c1-434e-989f-84bcc9029899","vaultid":null,"uId":"19ab0386-d449-418e-8dcf-a1484689e2c5","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"WIC","skill_desc":"Words in Context","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":1,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Many ancient sculptures of people&rsquo;s heads are missing their noses. This is because the nose is the most <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> part of a sculpture of a person&rsquo;s head. It is delicate and sticks out from the rest of the sculpture, making it especially easy to break.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"36454b0b-2a06-49a8-8d8a-0c1acbb18cf5","content":"<p>recognizable</p>"},{"id":"7ab8bc1c-441a-4d5a-9fdf-22c6932aa039","content":"<p>fragile</p>"},{"id":"e1925cd8-98b2-4e59-82f6-e064cace2ab2","content":"<p>common</p>"},{"id":"3066a402-2f77-4c5c-bec1-c48f6e6d51db","content":"<p>sophisticated</p>"}],"keys":["7ab8bc1c-441a-4d5a-9fdf-22c6932aa039"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of noses on ancient sculptures. In this context, \"fragile\" means weak or delicate. This matches the text&rsquo;s description of noses on ancient sculptures, which are often missing from sculptures&rsquo; heads because they are \"especially easy to break.\" Therefore, this context indicates that noses on ancient sculptures are the most fragile part of the sculptures&rsquo; heads.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. In this context, \"recognizable\" would mean identifiable, and since the text indicates that noses are often missing from ancient statues, they therefore cannot be the most recognizable part of the statue. Choice C is incorrect because the text indicates that many ancient statues are missing noses, so noses wouldn&rsquo;t be \"common,\" or frequent, aspects of ancient statues; they would conversely be uncommon. Choice D is incorrect because the text only indicates that noses on ancient statues often stick out and end up missing from the heads, which doesn&rsquo;t relate to the noses being \"sophisticated,\" or knowledgeable or refined.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323033,"updateDate":1730147323033}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323033, 1730147323033),
    ($SATQ$3f37eb3b$SATQ$, $SATQ$a70f7ce0-6502-4275-9474-263708c54dce$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Words in Context$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 5, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>People sometimes dismiss a claim if it comes from a source they regard as self-interested, but from a strictly logical perspective, the source of a claim is <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span>: it has no direct bearing on whether the claim is true. &nbsp;</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"aa476a0b-41cf-47d7-ab46-1d51070dc98b","content":"<p>indistinct</p>"},{"id":"51fe316c-d025-4bba-a828-5f007cbb7886","content":"<p>irrelevant</p>"},{"id":"23adc400-9285-4383-8c28-0d61adb91539","content":"<p>indisputable</p>"},{"id":"6adce41b-70c3-4938-a1ff-679d775742b3","content":"<p>implicit</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["51fe316c-d025-4bba-a828-5f007cbb7886"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion about people&rsquo;s tendency to dismiss claims from sources perceived as self-interested, or acting for their own advantage. In this context, "irrelevant" means not applicable to the matter at hand. The text explains that as a matter of logical reasoning, the source of a claim has nothing to do with the claim&rsquo;s truthfulness&mdash;a claim is either true or false in actuality, regardless of where it originates. This context suggests that even though people may distrust a claim based on its source, the source of the claim is actually irrelevant.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because in this context, "indistinct" would mean uncertain or not clearly recognizable. Instead of suggesting that the source of a claim can&rsquo;t be determined with certainty, the text suggests that recognizing a source and having an opinion of it simply doesn&rsquo;t matter because as a matter of logic, a claim is true or false in actuality, regardless of where it originates. Choice C is incorrect because in this context, "indisputable" would mean impossible to question or deny. Although the text suggests that it isn&rsquo;t logical to assume a claim is false just because its source appears to be self-interested, it doesn&rsquo;t go so far as to suggest that the source of a claim can&rsquo;t be questioned&mdash;the text instead makes the point that from a logical standpoint, the source of a claim doesn&rsquo;t matter because the claim is either true or false in and of itself. Choice D is incorrect because in this context, "implicit" would mean suggested or understood without being directly expressed. Nothing in the text suggests that logically, the source of a claim is only suggested; instead of addressing whether sources can be directly identified, the text focuses on the idea that sources don&rsquo;t matter because a claim is true or false in actuality, regardless of where it originates.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"3f37eb3b","external_id":"a70f7ce0-6502-4275-9474-263708c54dce","vaultid":null,"uId":"0f05a441-61c4-4989-9884-200343aa4ff1","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"WIC","skill_desc":"Words in Context","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":5,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>People sometimes dismiss a claim if it comes from a source they regard as self-interested, but from a strictly logical perspective, the source of a claim is <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span>: it has no direct bearing on whether the claim is true. &nbsp;</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"aa476a0b-41cf-47d7-ab46-1d51070dc98b","content":"<p>indistinct</p>"},{"id":"51fe316c-d025-4bba-a828-5f007cbb7886","content":"<p>irrelevant</p>"},{"id":"23adc400-9285-4383-8c28-0d61adb91539","content":"<p>indisputable</p>"},{"id":"6adce41b-70c3-4938-a1ff-679d775742b3","content":"<p>implicit</p>"}],"keys":["51fe316c-d025-4bba-a828-5f007cbb7886"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion about people&rsquo;s tendency to dismiss claims from sources perceived as self-interested, or acting for their own advantage. In this context, \"irrelevant\" means not applicable to the matter at hand. The text explains that as a matter of logical reasoning, the source of a claim has nothing to do with the claim&rsquo;s truthfulness&mdash;a claim is either true or false in actuality, regardless of where it originates. This context suggests that even though people may distrust a claim based on its source, the source of the claim is actually irrelevant.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because in this context, \"indistinct\" would mean uncertain or not clearly recognizable. Instead of suggesting that the source of a claim can&rsquo;t be determined with certainty, the text suggests that recognizing a source and having an opinion of it simply doesn&rsquo;t matter because as a matter of logic, a claim is true or false in actuality, regardless of where it originates. Choice C is incorrect because in this context, \"indisputable\" would mean impossible to question or deny. Although the text suggests that it isn&rsquo;t logical to assume a claim is false just because its source appears to be self-interested, it doesn&rsquo;t go so far as to suggest that the source of a claim can&rsquo;t be questioned&mdash;the text instead makes the point that from a logical standpoint, the source of a claim doesn&rsquo;t matter because the claim is either true or false in and of itself. Choice D is incorrect because in this context, \"implicit\" would mean suggested or understood without being directly expressed. Nothing in the text suggests that logically, the source of a claim is only suggested; instead of addressing whether sources can be directly identified, the text focuses on the idea that sources don&rsquo;t matter because a claim is true or false in actuality, regardless of where it originates.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1743430669212,"updateDate":1743430669212}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1743430669212, 1743430669212),
    ($SATQ$3f753a8e$SATQ$, $SATQ$24a7d56a-2465-41f9-a568-8027e878431d$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$7fdbb01f-1012-421d-9d3a-a82e613d4e0f$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Words in Context$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 6, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Investigating whether shared false visual memories&mdash;specific but inaccurate and widely held recollections of images such as product logos&mdash;are caused by people&rsquo;s previous <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> incorrect renditions of the images, researchers Deepasri Prasad and Wilma Bainbridge found that, in fact, such memories are often not explained by familiarity with erroneous versions of the images.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"ab5b2306-bbed-4ba4-93c1-2de83859e439","content":"<p>compliance with</p>"},{"id":"62e13083-bfe9-4339-885a-b3ec0a2b695c","content":"<p>exposure to</p>"},{"id":"408cc038-a9c5-4016-969d-762fd74d224e","content":"<p>criteria for</p>"},{"id":"823b6a7e-597f-4a84-9650-7bcd4a060606","content":"<p>forfeiture of</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["62e13083-bfe9-4339-885a-b3ec0a2b695c"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer. "Exposure to" means "having contact with." It makes sense that Prasad and Bainbridge were investigating whether seeing false versions of images was a cause of false visual memories. Notice how "exposure to incorrect renditions" matches the idea of "familiarity with erroneous versions," which appears later in the sentence.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. "Compliance with" means "going along with a command or directive." False versions of images can&rsquo;t give commands or directives, so this doesn&rsquo;t apply. Choice C is incorrect. "Criteria" means "standards by which to judge something." It&rsquo;s not clear how people would come to have standards for the wrong version of an image in the first place, let alone how those standards would cause them to falsely remember the correct version. In other words, this choice would result in a confusing, unclear sentence. Choice D is incorrect. "Forfeiture of" means "a giving up of something." It wouldn&rsquo;t make sense to say that false memories of an image might be caused by giving up the wrong version of the image.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-KA-WIC-H009$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$63e0e889-8a1f-4703-b36a-d85d7e651c34$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"3f753a8e","external_id":"24a7d56a-2465-41f9-a568-8027e878431d","vaultid":"7fdbb01f-1012-421d-9d3a-a82e613d4e0f","uId":"d1fe0c54-b83f-4b46-9a1d-4c167c4ef5e7","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"WIC","skill_desc":"Words in Context","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":6,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Investigating whether shared false visual memories&mdash;specific but inaccurate and widely held recollections of images such as product logos&mdash;are caused by people&rsquo;s previous <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> incorrect renditions of the images, researchers Deepasri Prasad and Wilma Bainbridge found that, in fact, such memories are often not explained by familiarity with erroneous versions of the images.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"ab5b2306-bbed-4ba4-93c1-2de83859e439","content":"<p>compliance with</p>"},{"id":"62e13083-bfe9-4339-885a-b3ec0a2b695c","content":"<p>exposure to</p>"},{"id":"408cc038-a9c5-4016-969d-762fd74d224e","content":"<p>criteria for</p>"},{"id":"823b6a7e-597f-4a84-9650-7bcd4a060606","content":"<p>forfeiture of</p>"}],"keys":["62e13083-bfe9-4339-885a-b3ec0a2b695c"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer. \"Exposure to\" means \"having contact with.\" It makes sense that Prasad and Bainbridge were investigating whether seeing false versions of images was a cause of false visual memories. Notice how \"exposure to incorrect renditions\" matches the idea of \"familiarity with erroneous versions,\" which appears later in the sentence.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. \"Compliance with\" means \"going along with a command or directive.\" False versions of images can&rsquo;t give commands or directives, so this doesn&rsquo;t apply. Choice C is incorrect. \"Criteria\" means \"standards by which to judge something.\" It&rsquo;s not clear how people would come to have standards for the wrong version of an image in the first place, let alone how those standards would cause them to falsely remember the correct version. In other words, this choice would result in a confusing, unclear sentence. Choice D is incorrect. \"Forfeiture of\" means \"a giving up of something.\" It wouldn&rsquo;t make sense to say that false memories of an image might be caused by giving up the wrong version of the image.</p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-KA-WIC-H009","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"63e0e889-8a1f-4703-b36a-d85d7e651c34","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959378,"updateDate":1691007959378}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959378, 1691007959378),
    ($SATQ$40270820$SATQ$, $SATQ$d216099d-d5d3-4506-a9ac-2eeb13341864$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$TSP$SATQ$, $SATQ$Text Structure and Purpose$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Catherine L. Cardel&uacute;s is a researcher who studies canopy soil. Canopy soil is formed in a tree&rsquo;s branches (its canopy) when dead leaves and other falling things collect. This material breaks down, becoming canopy soil. Canopy soil helps preserve a healthy water cycle (how water moves through the environment) in rainforests. This benefit is one reason Cardel&uacute;s is interested in the canopy soils in large choib&aacute; trees in rainforests in Costa Rica.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice best describes the text&rsquo;s overall structure?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"44b1082f-98d8-4311-ae3e-bbfd19b7a6e3","content":"<p>It explains a disagreement between researchers and how it was resolved.</p>"},{"id":"0623fe32-8099-4440-82a1-47524388984e","content":"<p>It presents an idea and mentions a researcher who disagrees with that idea.</p>"},{"id":"0db32c8d-0458-41af-8e54-1f695a1df494","content":"<p>It compares the sizes of two types of trees over several years.</p>"},{"id":"43abb6c2-c6f9-4429-bdd2-782e8e94c079","content":"<p>It introduces a researcher and gives information about what she researches.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["43abb6c2-c6f9-4429-bdd2-782e8e94c079"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer because it most accurately describes the overall structure of the text. The text begins by mentioning Catherine L. Cardel&uacute;s, "a researcher who studies canopy soil." The text then provides information about her chosen research topic: the formation of canopy soil in tree branches, the role of canopy soil in preserving a healthy water cycle in rainforests, and Cardel&uacute;s&rsquo;s specific interest in "the canopy soils in large choib&aacute; trees in rainforests in Costa Rica." Thus, the text introduces a researcher and then gives information about what she researches.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the text doesn&rsquo;t mention any disagreement between researchers, nor does it describe how such a disagreement was resolved. Choice B is incorrect because the text doesn&rsquo;t present an idea that Cardel&uacute;s disagrees with; rather, it describes her research interests and the significance of canopy soil. Choice C is incorrect because the text doesn&rsquo;t compare the sizes of different types of trees over time. While the text mentions "large choib&aacute; trees," it doesn&rsquo;t compare them to any other tree type or discuss changes in tree size over several years.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"40270820","external_id":"d216099d-d5d3-4506-a9ac-2eeb13341864","vaultid":null,"uId":"fbf9124c-da88-4779-9ace-37fb7407f43a","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"TSP","skill_desc":"Text Structure and Purpose","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":2,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Catherine L. Cardel&uacute;s is a researcher who studies canopy soil. Canopy soil is formed in a tree&rsquo;s branches (its canopy) when dead leaves and other falling things collect. This material breaks down, becoming canopy soil. Canopy soil helps preserve a healthy water cycle (how water moves through the environment) in rainforests. This benefit is one reason Cardel&uacute;s is interested in the canopy soils in large choib&aacute; trees in rainforests in Costa Rica.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice best describes the text&rsquo;s overall structure?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"44b1082f-98d8-4311-ae3e-bbfd19b7a6e3","content":"<p>It explains a disagreement between researchers and how it was resolved.</p>"},{"id":"0623fe32-8099-4440-82a1-47524388984e","content":"<p>It presents an idea and mentions a researcher who disagrees with that idea.</p>"},{"id":"0db32c8d-0458-41af-8e54-1f695a1df494","content":"<p>It compares the sizes of two types of trees over several years.</p>"},{"id":"43abb6c2-c6f9-4429-bdd2-782e8e94c079","content":"<p>It introduces a researcher and gives information about what she researches.</p>"}],"keys":["43abb6c2-c6f9-4429-bdd2-782e8e94c079"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer because it most accurately describes the overall structure of the text. The text begins by mentioning Catherine L. Cardel&uacute;s, \"a researcher who studies canopy soil.\" The text then provides information about her chosen research topic: the formation of canopy soil in tree branches, the role of canopy soil in preserving a healthy water cycle in rainforests, and Cardel&uacute;s&rsquo;s specific interest in \"the canopy soils in large choib&aacute; trees in rainforests in Costa Rica.\" Thus, the text introduces a researcher and then gives information about what she researches.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the text doesn&rsquo;t mention any disagreement between researchers, nor does it describe how such a disagreement was resolved. Choice B is incorrect because the text doesn&rsquo;t present an idea that Cardel&uacute;s disagrees with; rather, it describes her research interests and the significance of canopy soil. Choice C is incorrect because the text doesn&rsquo;t compare the sizes of different types of trees over time. While the text mentions \"large choib&aacute; trees,\" it doesn&rsquo;t compare them to any other tree type or discuss changes in tree size over several years.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1755117061192,"updateDate":1755117061192}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1755117061192, 1755117061192),
    ($SATQ$417f8dfa$SATQ$, $SATQ$7a2c8836-7561-41d9-a391-1a2361cad7b6$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Words in Context$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The following text is from Darcie Little Badger&rsquo;s 2021 novel <em>A Snake Falls to Earth</em>. Nina is looking at old family photographs.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Nina couldn&rsquo;t stop looking at the sepia-toned photograph in an oak frame. Propped on the window ledge, it <span style="text-decoration: underline;" role="region" aria-label="Referenced Content">featured</span> a portrait of Great-Great-Grandmother Rosita as a young woman.</p>
</blockquote>
<p style="text-align: right;"><span class="dap_copyright">&copy;2021 by Darcie Little Badger</span></p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>As used in the text, what does the word &ldquo;featured&rdquo; most nearly mean?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"f95cc719-61df-4a03-8d95-d25600cc54c0","content":"<p>Displayed</p>"},{"id":"f6dbee95-1e86-46c2-ae6a-856930c12e99","content":"<p>Questioned</p>"},{"id":"4451b81d-7e46-4b5f-9974-31b904616572","content":"<p>Approved</p>"},{"id":"2ccbfb24-6984-4aa2-a9e1-76f7c7b5f0a6","content":"<p>Ignored</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["f95cc719-61df-4a03-8d95-d25600cc54c0"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer because as used in the text, "featured" most nearly means displayed. The text describes Nina&rsquo;s fascination with a framed photograph. It then goes on to describe what the photograph showed, or displayed, which is a portrait of Nina&rsquo;s great-great-grandmother Rosita as a young woman. Thus, the text indicates that the photograph displayed a portrait of Rosita.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect because the text identifies the person shown in the photograph as Nina&rsquo;s great-great-grandmother; it gives no indication that the contents of the photograph were questioned, or asked about. Choice C is incorrect because approved means expressed a favorable opinion of, and there&rsquo;s nothing to suggest that the photograph of Nina&rsquo;s great-great-grandmother has received approval or been viewed favorably, only that Nina is unable to stop looking at it. Choice D is incorrect because nothing in the text suggests that the photograph Nina is fascinated with ignored, or refused to take notice of, Nina&rsquo;s great-great-grandmother Rosita. Rather, the text indicates that the photograph was a portrait of Rosita.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"417f8dfa","external_id":"7a2c8836-7561-41d9-a391-1a2361cad7b6","vaultid":null,"uId":"7e5795f3-d1fa-40d7-8ec7-65b5ce30df27","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"WIC","skill_desc":"Words in Context","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":2,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>The following text is from Darcie Little Badger&rsquo;s 2021 novel <em>A Snake Falls to Earth</em>. Nina is looking at old family photographs.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Nina couldn&rsquo;t stop looking at the sepia-toned photograph in an oak frame. Propped on the window ledge, it <span style=\"text-decoration: underline;\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Referenced Content\">featured</span> a portrait of Great-Great-Grandmother Rosita as a young woman.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p style=\"text-align: right;\"><span class=\"dap_copyright\">&copy;2021 by Darcie Little Badger</span></p>","stem":"<p>As used in the text, what does the word &ldquo;featured&rdquo; most nearly mean?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"f95cc719-61df-4a03-8d95-d25600cc54c0","content":"<p>Displayed</p>"},{"id":"f6dbee95-1e86-46c2-ae6a-856930c12e99","content":"<p>Questioned</p>"},{"id":"4451b81d-7e46-4b5f-9974-31b904616572","content":"<p>Approved</p>"},{"id":"2ccbfb24-6984-4aa2-a9e1-76f7c7b5f0a6","content":"<p>Ignored</p>"}],"keys":["f95cc719-61df-4a03-8d95-d25600cc54c0"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer because as used in the text, \"featured\" most nearly means displayed. The text describes Nina&rsquo;s fascination with a framed photograph. It then goes on to describe what the photograph showed, or displayed, which is a portrait of Nina&rsquo;s great-great-grandmother Rosita as a young woman. Thus, the text indicates that the photograph displayed a portrait of Rosita.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect because the text identifies the person shown in the photograph as Nina&rsquo;s great-great-grandmother; it gives no indication that the contents of the photograph were questioned, or asked about. Choice C is incorrect because approved means expressed a favorable opinion of, and there&rsquo;s nothing to suggest that the photograph of Nina&rsquo;s great-great-grandmother has received approval or been viewed favorably, only that Nina is unable to stop looking at it. Choice D is incorrect because nothing in the text suggests that the photograph Nina is fascinated with ignored, or refused to take notice of, Nina&rsquo;s great-great-grandmother Rosita. Rather, the text indicates that the photograph was a portrait of Rosita.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323052,"updateDate":1730147323052}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323052, 1730147323052),
    ($SATQ$422c5068$SATQ$, $SATQ$231583a6-b3eb-41cf-bc8a-41e1fac75709$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$TSP$SATQ$, $SATQ$Text Structure and Purpose$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Generally it takes Tule geese about four days to migrate south for the winter. From their summer breeding grounds in Cook Inlet, Alaska, <span style="text-decoration: underline;" role="region" aria-label="Referenced Content">the birds begin by flying over the Gulf of Alaska, keeping about 100 miles from the Canadian shore</span>. They pause to rest on the Pacific Ocean, then fly toward Summer Lake, Oregon, before finally arriving at their winter destination of Sacramento Valley, California. In 2020, however, it took the geese over twice as long to make their way from Cook Inlet to Sacramento Valley. According to researchers, the reason was airborne pollutants.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice best states the function of the underlined portion in the text as a whole?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"4a277424-aef5-49a0-bc07-b6147ad67ce0","content":"<p>It illustrates a change in Tule geese&rsquo;s usual flight behavior.</p>"},{"id":"9bc8c89f-a3da-44ec-b65b-60c29b725d83","content":"<p>It suggests an explanation for why Tule geese breed in Alaska.</p>"},{"id":"a5dde204-3547-41d5-b15f-cbbe2c63167e","content":"<p>It describes part of the Tule geese&rsquo;s typical winter migration journey.</p>"},{"id":"24994f76-cd53-4905-ba41-3df3837a3b99","content":"<p>It compares Tule geese to other birds that migrate south for the winter.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["a5dde204-3547-41d5-b15f-cbbe2c63167e"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer because it best states how the underlined portion functions in the text as a whole. The first sentence states that Tule geese typically take four days to migrate south. Then in the next sentence, which contains the underlined portion, the text describes the first part of that journey, which begins with the geese flying over the Gulf of Alaska while keeping about 100 miles from the Canadian shore. The rest of the text details the remainder of the geese&rsquo;s typical journey and then mentions circumstances in 2020 that resulted in the geese taking an unusually long time to complete it. Thus, the underlined portion describes part of the Tule geese&rsquo;s typical winter migration journey.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. Although the text concludes by stating that in 2020, Tule geese took twice as long to complete their typical migration, which suggests a change in their usual flight behavior, the underlined portion doesn&rsquo;t discuss this. Instead, the underlined portion describes the first part of the Tule geese&rsquo;s typical winter migration journey over the Gulf of Alaska. Choice B is incorrect. Although the sentence containing the underlined portion mentions that Tule geese breed in Alaska, the underlined portion doesn&rsquo;t explain why the geese breed in that location. Rather, the text mentions Alaska to explain that it&rsquo;s the starting point of the Tule geese&rsquo;s typical winter migration. Choice D is incorrect because the underlined portion doesn&rsquo;t discuss any other birds that migrate south for the winter. In fact, the text is only concerned with the migration of Tule geese.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"422c5068","external_id":"231583a6-b3eb-41cf-bc8a-41e1fac75709","vaultid":null,"uId":"79335607-3a0f-4eeb-8346-917a62740507","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"TSP","skill_desc":"Text Structure and Purpose","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Generally it takes Tule geese about four days to migrate south for the winter. From their summer breeding grounds in Cook Inlet, Alaska, <span style=\"text-decoration: underline;\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Referenced Content\">the birds begin by flying over the Gulf of Alaska, keeping about 100 miles from the Canadian shore</span>. They pause to rest on the Pacific Ocean, then fly toward Summer Lake, Oregon, before finally arriving at their winter destination of Sacramento Valley, California. In 2020, however, it took the geese over twice as long to make their way from Cook Inlet to Sacramento Valley. According to researchers, the reason was airborne pollutants.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice best states the function of the underlined portion in the text as a whole?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"4a277424-aef5-49a0-bc07-b6147ad67ce0","content":"<p>It illustrates a change in Tule geese&rsquo;s usual flight behavior.</p>"},{"id":"9bc8c89f-a3da-44ec-b65b-60c29b725d83","content":"<p>It suggests an explanation for why Tule geese breed in Alaska.</p>"},{"id":"a5dde204-3547-41d5-b15f-cbbe2c63167e","content":"<p>It describes part of the Tule geese&rsquo;s typical winter migration journey.</p>"},{"id":"24994f76-cd53-4905-ba41-3df3837a3b99","content":"<p>It compares Tule geese to other birds that migrate south for the winter.</p>"}],"keys":["a5dde204-3547-41d5-b15f-cbbe2c63167e"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer because it best states how the underlined portion functions in the text as a whole. The first sentence states that Tule geese typically take four days to migrate south. Then in the next sentence, which contains the underlined portion, the text describes the first part of that journey, which begins with the geese flying over the Gulf of Alaska while keeping about 100 miles from the Canadian shore. The rest of the text details the remainder of the geese&rsquo;s typical journey and then mentions circumstances in 2020 that resulted in the geese taking an unusually long time to complete it. Thus, the underlined portion describes part of the Tule geese&rsquo;s typical winter migration journey.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. Although the text concludes by stating that in 2020, Tule geese took twice as long to complete their typical migration, which suggests a change in their usual flight behavior, the underlined portion doesn&rsquo;t discuss this. Instead, the underlined portion describes the first part of the Tule geese&rsquo;s typical winter migration journey over the Gulf of Alaska. Choice B is incorrect. Although the sentence containing the underlined portion mentions that Tule geese breed in Alaska, the underlined portion doesn&rsquo;t explain why the geese breed in that location. Rather, the text mentions Alaska to explain that it&rsquo;s the starting point of the Tule geese&rsquo;s typical winter migration. Choice D is incorrect because the underlined portion doesn&rsquo;t discuss any other birds that migrate south for the winter. In fact, the text is only concerned with the migration of Tule geese.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323041,"updateDate":1730147323041}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323041, 1730147323041),
    ($SATQ$428cd2c1$SATQ$, $SATQ$f522b365-f853-4553-b809-e6b3752f1e5a$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$487d418d-9a1a-4c02-862f-eeddce0f9db3$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Words in Context$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 3, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Some people have speculated that two helmets with attached horns discovered in Denmark in 1942 belonged to Vikings, but scholars have long been skeptical. Archaeologist Helle Vandkilde and colleagues recently provided radiocarbon dates for the helmets, and their findings <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> scholars&rsquo; skepticism: the helmets date to the Nordic Bronze Age, centuries before the Vikings existed.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"a999b0c3-61be-4194-9435-4ac90f8a9be4","content":"<p>anticipate</p>"},{"id":"370dc8b8-b4cd-4b7d-baca-1fb1107d4896","content":"<p>inspect</p>"},{"id":"73ceb23f-37cb-4a6d-b04b-45fa718f0392","content":"<p>reveal</p>"},{"id":"d8fff84c-a5c7-4c30-8b83-5f91278fa690","content":"<p>justify</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["d8fff84c-a5c7-4c30-8b83-5f91278fa690"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of the helmets found in Denmark. In this context, &ldquo;justify&rdquo; means confirm or give reasons for. The text indicates that scholars have long been skeptical about the supposed Viking origin of two helmets found in Denmark. The radiocarbon dating of the helmets conducted by Vandkilde and colleagues demonstrates that the helmets date from the Nordic Bronze Age, making the helmets too old to have belonged to Vikings. This context supports the idea that the scholars&rsquo; skepticism is justified. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because &ldquo;anticipate&rdquo; means expect or come before, neither of which would make sense in context. The text indicates that scholars have long been skeptical of the idea that the helmets belonged to Vikings. Because the skepticism has existed for a long time, Vandkilde and colleagues&rsquo; research couldn&rsquo;t be said to anticipate it. Instead, the radiocarbon dating results justify or confirm the scholar&rsquo;s skepticism. Choice B is incorrect because &ldquo;inspect&rdquo; means examine or review, which wouldn&rsquo;t make sense in this context. Research findings are inanimate and therefore unable to inspect scholars&rsquo; skepticism. The text focuses on the origin of two helmets, which some people believe belonged to Vikings. Vandkilde and colleagues found that the helmets are from the Nordic Bronze Age and therefore much older than the Vikings. This context suggests that the researchers&rsquo; findings justify, not inspect, the scholars&rsquo; skepticism. Choice C is incorrect because &ldquo;reveal&rdquo; means uncover or report, which wouldn&rsquo;t make sense in context. The text focuses on the origin of two helmets, which some people believe belonged to Vikings. Vandkilde and colleagues tested the helmets and found that they date to the Nordic Bronze Age and therefore are much older than the Vikings. This context suggests that the researchers&rsquo; findings confirm or justify the scholars&rsquo; skepticism, which was already known so didn&rsquo;t need to be revealed. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-021-WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$9eff37d2-9604-475e-a39d-de8dc0414109$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"428cd2c1","external_id":"f522b365-f853-4553-b809-e6b3752f1e5a","vaultid":"487d418d-9a1a-4c02-862f-eeddce0f9db3","uId":"b949b929-7adc-4c7d-b4b8-a6088108e29d","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"WIC","skill_desc":"Words in Context","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":3,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Some people have speculated that two helmets with attached horns discovered in Denmark in 1942 belonged to Vikings, but scholars have long been skeptical. Archaeologist Helle Vandkilde and colleagues recently provided radiocarbon dates for the helmets, and their findings <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> scholars&rsquo; skepticism: the helmets date to the Nordic Bronze Age, centuries before the Vikings existed.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"a999b0c3-61be-4194-9435-4ac90f8a9be4","content":"<p>anticipate</p>"},{"id":"370dc8b8-b4cd-4b7d-baca-1fb1107d4896","content":"<p>inspect</p>"},{"id":"73ceb23f-37cb-4a6d-b04b-45fa718f0392","content":"<p>reveal</p>"},{"id":"d8fff84c-a5c7-4c30-8b83-5f91278fa690","content":"<p>justify</p>"}],"keys":["d8fff84c-a5c7-4c30-8b83-5f91278fa690"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of the helmets found in Denmark. In this context, &ldquo;justify&rdquo; means confirm or give reasons for. The text indicates that scholars have long been skeptical about the supposed Viking origin of two helmets found in Denmark. The radiocarbon dating of the helmets conducted by Vandkilde and colleagues demonstrates that the helmets date from the Nordic Bronze Age, making the helmets too old to have belonged to Vikings. This context supports the idea that the scholars&rsquo; skepticism is justified. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because &ldquo;anticipate&rdquo; means expect or come before, neither of which would make sense in context. The text indicates that scholars have long been skeptical of the idea that the helmets belonged to Vikings. Because the skepticism has existed for a long time, Vandkilde and colleagues&rsquo; research couldn&rsquo;t be said to anticipate it. Instead, the radiocarbon dating results justify or confirm the scholar&rsquo;s skepticism. Choice B is incorrect because &ldquo;inspect&rdquo; means examine or review, which wouldn&rsquo;t make sense in this context. Research findings are inanimate and therefore unable to inspect scholars&rsquo; skepticism. The text focuses on the origin of two helmets, which some people believe belonged to Vikings. Vandkilde and colleagues found that the helmets are from the Nordic Bronze Age and therefore much older than the Vikings. This context suggests that the researchers&rsquo; findings justify, not inspect, the scholars&rsquo; skepticism. Choice C is incorrect because &ldquo;reveal&rdquo; means uncover or report, which wouldn&rsquo;t make sense in context. The text focuses on the origin of two helmets, which some people believe belonged to Vikings. Vandkilde and colleagues tested the helmets and found that they date to the Nordic Bronze Age and therefore are much older than the Vikings. This context suggests that the researchers&rsquo; findings confirm or justify the scholars&rsquo; skepticism, which was already known so didn&rsquo;t need to be revealed. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-021-WIC","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"9eff37d2-9604-475e-a39d-de8dc0414109","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959317,"updateDate":1691007959317}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959317, 1691007959317),
    ($SATQ$441e2b9e$SATQ$, $SATQ$38e385c3-7997-49a3-a7be-4b795810f519$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$d62cf830-ecc4-48c2-821d-f26636a9d056$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Words in Context$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 3, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Researchers and conservationists stress that biodiversity loss due to invasive species is <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span>. For example, people can take simple steps such as washing their footwear after travel to avoid introducing potentially invasive organisms into new environments.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"266fae83-f9ac-41a6-9915-caab2a7e55d6","content":"<p>preventable</p>"},{"id":"4e0df63d-331c-4f5c-8a33-14264c7b48f9","content":"<p>undeniable</p>"},{"id":"3b8c5ff0-a6a6-4f57-9777-ae6cc6d0505c","content":"<p>common</p>"},{"id":"484d3360-220e-40ea-acd7-97fce74edc8e","content":"<p>concerning</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["266fae83-f9ac-41a6-9915-caab2a7e55d6"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of how biodiversity loss due to invasive species can be avoided. As used in this context, &ldquo;preventable&rdquo; means able to be stopped or kept from happening. The text indicates that &ldquo;people can take simple steps&rdquo; to avoid bringing possible invasive species into new environments. It presents these steps as an example of how biodiversity loss due to invasive species is preventable. </p><p>Choice B is incorrect because it wouldn&rsquo;t make sense to say that a simple step like washing your shoes after traveling is an example of biodiversity loss due to invasive species being &ldquo;undeniable,&rdquo; or something that can&rsquo;t be proved to be wrong. Although the text may suggest that biodiversity loss due to invasive species is something that really happens, the word that completes the text must make the first sentence into an assertion that is illustrated by the second sentence, and the second sentence illustrates the idea that biodiversity loss due to invasive species is preventable, not undeniable. Choice C is incorrect because it wouldn&rsquo;t make sense to say that a simple step like washing your shoes after traveling is an example of biodiversity loss due to invasive species being &ldquo;common,&rdquo; or something that happens regularly. Additionally, the text doesn&rsquo;t provide any information about how frequently invasive species cause biodiversity loss.&nbsp;Choice D is incorrect because it wouldn&rsquo;t make sense to say that a simple step like washing your shoes after traveling is an example of biodiversity loss due to invasive species being &ldquo;concerning,&rdquo; or something that is troubling or causes worry. Although the text implies that the phenomenon of biodiversity loss due to invasive species is itself a concerning phenomenon, the word that completes the text must make the first sentence into an assertion that is illustrated by the second sentence, and the second sentence illustrates the idea that biodiversity loss due to invasive species is preventable, not concerning. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-955-WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$00ff1620-8df9-40d2-ac56-92dbf8a9a342$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"441e2b9e","external_id":"38e385c3-7997-49a3-a7be-4b795810f519","vaultid":"d62cf830-ecc4-48c2-821d-f26636a9d056","uId":"f6712ff3-212e-4df2-a729-586b339f61be","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"WIC","skill_desc":"Words in Context","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":3,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Researchers and conservationists stress that biodiversity loss due to invasive species is <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span>. For example, people can take simple steps such as washing their footwear after travel to avoid introducing potentially invasive organisms into new environments.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"266fae83-f9ac-41a6-9915-caab2a7e55d6","content":"<p>preventable</p>"},{"id":"4e0df63d-331c-4f5c-8a33-14264c7b48f9","content":"<p>undeniable</p>"},{"id":"3b8c5ff0-a6a6-4f57-9777-ae6cc6d0505c","content":"<p>common</p>"},{"id":"484d3360-220e-40ea-acd7-97fce74edc8e","content":"<p>concerning</p>"}],"keys":["266fae83-f9ac-41a6-9915-caab2a7e55d6"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of how biodiversity loss due to invasive species can be avoided. As used in this context, &ldquo;preventable&rdquo; means able to be stopped or kept from happening. The text indicates that &ldquo;people can take simple steps&rdquo; to avoid bringing possible invasive species into new environments. It presents these steps as an example of how biodiversity loss due to invasive species is preventable. </p><p>Choice B is incorrect because it wouldn&rsquo;t make sense to say that a simple step like washing your shoes after traveling is an example of biodiversity loss due to invasive species being &ldquo;undeniable,&rdquo; or something that can&rsquo;t be proved to be wrong. Although the text may suggest that biodiversity loss due to invasive species is something that really happens, the word that completes the text must make the first sentence into an assertion that is illustrated by the second sentence, and the second sentence illustrates the idea that biodiversity loss due to invasive species is preventable, not undeniable. Choice C is incorrect because it wouldn&rsquo;t make sense to say that a simple step like washing your shoes after traveling is an example of biodiversity loss due to invasive species being &ldquo;common,&rdquo; or something that happens regularly. Additionally, the text doesn&rsquo;t provide any information about how frequently invasive species cause biodiversity loss.&nbsp;Choice D is incorrect because it wouldn&rsquo;t make sense to say that a simple step like washing your shoes after traveling is an example of biodiversity loss due to invasive species being &ldquo;concerning,&rdquo; or something that is troubling or causes worry. Although the text implies that the phenomenon of biodiversity loss due to invasive species is itself a concerning phenomenon, the word that completes the text must make the first sentence into an assertion that is illustrated by the second sentence, and the second sentence illustrates the idea that biodiversity loss due to invasive species is preventable, not concerning. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-955-WIC","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"00ff1620-8df9-40d2-ac56-92dbf8a9a342","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959356,"updateDate":1691007959356}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959356, 1691007959356),
    ($SATQ$4480fae9$SATQ$, $SATQ$cb6b6b1b-7743-4c58-a006-f4432bbd11f8$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$51ba5a10-12f9-498a-b333-269b3dadf673$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Words in Context$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The following text is adapted from Sui Sin Far&rsquo;s 1912 short story &ldquo;Mrs. Spring Fragrance.&rdquo; Mr. and Mrs. Spring Fragrance immigrated to the United States from China.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Mrs. Spring Fragrance was unaware that Mr. Spring Fragrance, tired with the day&rsquo;s business, had thrown himself down on the bamboo settee on the veranda, and that although his eyes were engaged in scanning the pages of the&nbsp;<em>Chinese World</em>, his ears could not help <span role="region" aria-label="Referenced Content"><u>receiving</u></span> the words which were borne to him through the open window.</p>
</blockquote>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>As used in the text, what does the word &ldquo;receiving&rdquo; most nearly mean?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"10ac40e9-9467-45d3-9f2d-4156041f2b42","content":"<p>Denying</p>"},{"id":"c103f96d-ca12-4f9a-93cf-05619fcc3d21","content":"<p>Entering</p>"},{"id":"a12cea14-6b90-4538-87b3-1c4448b088be","content":"<p>Carrying</p>"},{"id":"d393b431-20e8-4478-b88a-1806501b57fc","content":"<p>Hearing</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["d393b431-20e8-4478-b88a-1806501b57fc"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer because as used in the text, &ldquo;receiving&rdquo; most nearly means hearing, or perceiving sound. The text describes Mr. Spring Fragrance as he reads a newspaper, focusing on his eyes and ears. While his eyes look at the newspaper, he is unwillingly distracted by words coming through a nearby open window (that is, &ldquo;his ears could not help receiving the words&rdquo;). The words are being perceived specifically by his ears and not his eyes, and ears are the organs of sense with which one hears, so therefore Mr. Spring Fragrance is hearing the words through the open window. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because there is no indication in the text that Mr. Spring Fragrance is denying, or refusing the truth of, the words coming to him through the window. He is merely distracted by them. Choice B is incorrect because it wouldn&rsquo;t make sense to say that Mr. Spring Fragrance&rsquo;s ears couldn&rsquo;t help entering, or coming into, the words coming to him through the window. Instead, the text indicates that the opposite occurs: that is, when Mr. Spring Fragrance hears the words, they enter his ears, but his ears don&rsquo;t enter the words. Choice C is incorrect because it doesn&rsquo;t make sense in this context to think of Mr. Spring Fragrance as carrying the words coming to him through the window, even though the word &ldquo;carry&rdquo; can have a number of different meanings depending on context: for example, he is not lifting them, he is not supporting them, and he is not accepting blame for them (as one &ldquo;carries the blame&rdquo; for a mistake). </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-037-WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$a8f8031b-e43f-4d09-9be0-b1af60c3b51a$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"4480fae9","external_id":"cb6b6b1b-7743-4c58-a006-f4432bbd11f8","vaultid":"51ba5a10-12f9-498a-b333-269b3dadf673","uId":"50eecf64-ec5b-4f92-a750-e311343f7fd4","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"WIC","skill_desc":"Words in Context","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":2,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>The following text is adapted from Sui Sin Far&rsquo;s 1912 short story &ldquo;Mrs. Spring Fragrance.&rdquo; Mr. and Mrs. Spring Fragrance immigrated to the United States from China.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Mrs. Spring Fragrance was unaware that Mr. Spring Fragrance, tired with the day&rsquo;s business, had thrown himself down on the bamboo settee on the veranda, and that although his eyes were engaged in scanning the pages of the&nbsp;<em>Chinese World</em>, his ears could not help <span role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Referenced Content\"><u>receiving</u></span> the words which were borne to him through the open window.</p>\n</blockquote>","stem":"<p>As used in the text, what does the word &ldquo;receiving&rdquo; most nearly mean?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"10ac40e9-9467-45d3-9f2d-4156041f2b42","content":"<p>Denying</p>"},{"id":"c103f96d-ca12-4f9a-93cf-05619fcc3d21","content":"<p>Entering</p>"},{"id":"a12cea14-6b90-4538-87b3-1c4448b088be","content":"<p>Carrying</p>"},{"id":"d393b431-20e8-4478-b88a-1806501b57fc","content":"<p>Hearing</p>"}],"keys":["d393b431-20e8-4478-b88a-1806501b57fc"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer because as used in the text, &ldquo;receiving&rdquo; most nearly means hearing, or perceiving sound. The text describes Mr. Spring Fragrance as he reads a newspaper, focusing on his eyes and ears. While his eyes look at the newspaper, he is unwillingly distracted by words coming through a nearby open window (that is, &ldquo;his ears could not help receiving the words&rdquo;). The words are being perceived specifically by his ears and not his eyes, and ears are the organs of sense with which one hears, so therefore Mr. Spring Fragrance is hearing the words through the open window. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because there is no indication in the text that Mr. Spring Fragrance is denying, or refusing the truth of, the words coming to him through the window. He is merely distracted by them. Choice B is incorrect because it wouldn&rsquo;t make sense to say that Mr. Spring Fragrance&rsquo;s ears couldn&rsquo;t help entering, or coming into, the words coming to him through the window. Instead, the text indicates that the opposite occurs: that is, when Mr. Spring Fragrance hears the words, they enter his ears, but his ears don&rsquo;t enter the words. Choice C is incorrect because it doesn&rsquo;t make sense in this context to think of Mr. Spring Fragrance as carrying the words coming to him through the window, even though the word &ldquo;carry&rdquo; can have a number of different meanings depending on context: for example, he is not lifting them, he is not supporting them, and he is not accepting blame for them (as one &ldquo;carries the blame&rdquo; for a mistake). </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-037-WIC","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"a8f8031b-e43f-4d09-9be0-b1af60c3b51a","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959321,"updateDate":1691007959321}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959321, 1691007959321),
    ($SATQ$44cc5f75$SATQ$, $SATQ$cea065bc-7b0e-462f-becd-f5577217fd37$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$cb02357d-ded1-442c-938b-5b0b08674b74$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Words in Context$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 3, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Artificially delivering biomolecules to plant cells is an important component of protecting plants from pathogens, but it is difficult to transmit biomolecules through the layers of the plant cell wall. Markita del Carpio Landry and her colleagues have shown that it may be possible to <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> this problem by transmitting molecules through carbon nanotubes, which can cross cell walls.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"17331b1d-30df-43c7-a8ee-5c4134c318f6","content":"<p>conceptualize</p>"},{"id":"12cb0ca3-f62b-4bbf-9f4e-a4fbfb26f62b","content":"<p>neglect</p>"},{"id":"5f620aad-6044-4e12-8d6a-68853cab2029","content":"<p>illustrate</p>"},{"id":"2752dad7-693a-4888-9d7c-147b9638042e","content":"<p>overcome</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["2752dad7-693a-4888-9d7c-147b9638042e"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of delivering biomolecules to plant cells. In this context, &ldquo;overcome&rdquo; means to succeed in dealing with an obstacle. The text suggests that although it&rsquo;s difficult to move biomolecules through plant cell walls, Landry and her colleagues have shown that carbon nanotubes may be useful, since they can cross cell walls. This context conveys that Landry and her colleagues think it&rsquo;s possible, using carbon nanotubes, to succeed in dealing with the obstacle of transmitting biomolecules to plant cells. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because it wouldn&rsquo;t make sense in context to say that Landry and her colleagues have shown that it may be possible to &ldquo;conceptualize,&rdquo; or form an idea of, the difficulty of transmitting biomolecules through the walls of plant cells. The text presents this difficulty as a known problem that Landry and her colleagues think they may have solved, not as a mysterious occurrence that they have yet to form ideas about. Choice B is incorrect because the text suggests that Landry and her colleagues think it may be possible to successfully deal with the problem of transmitting biomolecules through the walls of plant cells, not that Landry and her colleagues think it may be possible to &ldquo;neglect,&rdquo; or simply to disregard and ignore the problem. Choice C is incorrect because it wouldn&rsquo;t make sense in context to say that Landry and her colleagues have shown that it may be possible to &ldquo;illustrate,&rdquo; or demonstrate, the difficulty of transmitting biomolecules through the walls of plant cells by using carbon nanotubes. According to the text, carbon nanotubes allow molecules to be transmitted to plant cells&mdash;something that is otherwise difficult to do. The text therefore presents carbon nanotubes as a way of possibly solving a problem, not as a means of demonstrating the problem. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-922-WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$7c1bdfa0-0b2f-455c-b1b6-ec41aab5d589$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"44cc5f75","external_id":"cea065bc-7b0e-462f-becd-f5577217fd37","vaultid":"cb02357d-ded1-442c-938b-5b0b08674b74","uId":"66c016e5-7510-429c-bcba-83b40f241484","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"WIC","skill_desc":"Words in Context","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":3,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Artificially delivering biomolecules to plant cells is an important component of protecting plants from pathogens, but it is difficult to transmit biomolecules through the layers of the plant cell wall. Markita del Carpio Landry and her colleagues have shown that it may be possible to <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> this problem by transmitting molecules through carbon nanotubes, which can cross cell walls.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"17331b1d-30df-43c7-a8ee-5c4134c318f6","content":"<p>conceptualize</p>"},{"id":"12cb0ca3-f62b-4bbf-9f4e-a4fbfb26f62b","content":"<p>neglect</p>"},{"id":"5f620aad-6044-4e12-8d6a-68853cab2029","content":"<p>illustrate</p>"},{"id":"2752dad7-693a-4888-9d7c-147b9638042e","content":"<p>overcome</p>"}],"keys":["2752dad7-693a-4888-9d7c-147b9638042e"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of delivering biomolecules to plant cells. In this context, &ldquo;overcome&rdquo; means to succeed in dealing with an obstacle. The text suggests that although it&rsquo;s difficult to move biomolecules through plant cell walls, Landry and her colleagues have shown that carbon nanotubes may be useful, since they can cross cell walls. This context conveys that Landry and her colleagues think it&rsquo;s possible, using carbon nanotubes, to succeed in dealing with the obstacle of transmitting biomolecules to plant cells. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because it wouldn&rsquo;t make sense in context to say that Landry and her colleagues have shown that it may be possible to &ldquo;conceptualize,&rdquo; or form an idea of, the difficulty of transmitting biomolecules through the walls of plant cells. The text presents this difficulty as a known problem that Landry and her colleagues think they may have solved, not as a mysterious occurrence that they have yet to form ideas about. Choice B is incorrect because the text suggests that Landry and her colleagues think it may be possible to successfully deal with the problem of transmitting biomolecules through the walls of plant cells, not that Landry and her colleagues think it may be possible to &ldquo;neglect,&rdquo; or simply to disregard and ignore the problem. Choice C is incorrect because it wouldn&rsquo;t make sense in context to say that Landry and her colleagues have shown that it may be possible to &ldquo;illustrate,&rdquo; or demonstrate, the difficulty of transmitting biomolecules through the walls of plant cells by using carbon nanotubes. According to the text, carbon nanotubes allow molecules to be transmitted to plant cells&mdash;something that is otherwise difficult to do. The text therefore presents carbon nanotubes as a way of possibly solving a problem, not as a means of demonstrating the problem. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-922-WIC","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"7c1bdfa0-0b2f-455c-b1b6-ec41aab5d589","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959355,"updateDate":1691007959355}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959355, 1691007959355),
    ($SATQ$45a109a3$SATQ$, $SATQ$622d080b-68f5-454f-8cb0-2a9f3be0c3f6$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$3abdef0e-8c1b-4a61-b0f9-f14d9a40ac06$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$Words in Context$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 3, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The following text is from Bram Stoker&rsquo;s 1897 novel <em>Dracula</em>. The narrator is being driven in a carriage through a remote region at night.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The baying of the wolves sounded nearer and nearer, as though they were closing round on us from every side. I grew dreadfully afraid, and the horses shared my fear. The driver, however, was not in the least <span role="region" aria-label="Referenced Content"><u>disturbed</u></span>; he kept turning his head to left and right, but I could not see anything through the darkness.</p>
</blockquote>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>As used in the text, what does the word &ldquo;disturbed&rdquo; most nearly mean?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"bcdb6b6b-d4e6-4c34-8628-9fb463ceecc1","content":"<p>Disorganized</p>"},{"id":"62943c2d-baeb-4873-b23a-07dda05d76d5","content":"<p>Alarmed</p>"},{"id":"6d7fc0bb-5c14-4215-b6b0-943f018199d5","content":"<p>Offended</p>"},{"id":"a36e6764-0ad8-40b8-a322-5a794b73e26d","content":"<p>Interrupted</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["62943c2d-baeb-4873-b23a-07dda05d76d5"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer because as used in the text, &ldquo;disturbed&rdquo; most nearly means alarmed. The text portrays the narrator traveling in a carriage as wolves howl in the surrounding darkness. The text contrasts the reaction of both the narrator and the horses pulling the carriage with that of the driver of the carriage: the narrator and horses are &ldquo;dreadfully afraid,&rdquo; but the driver is &ldquo;not in the least disturbed.&rdquo; In other words, the driver is not alarmed by the wolves nearby. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. Although in some contexts, &ldquo;disturbed&rdquo; can mean disorganized, the text doesn&rsquo;t portray a character acting in a disorganized manner; instead, the driver continues to drive the carriage, even though the horses pulling it are alarmed.&nbsp;Choice C is incorrect. Although in some contexts, &ldquo;disturbed&rdquo; can mean offended, the text doesn&rsquo;t portray one character feeling offended, or upset, by another&rsquo;s actions; instead, it contrasts the fear felt by the narrator with another character&rsquo;s lack of fear.&nbsp;Choice D is incorrect. Although in some contexts, &ldquo;disturbed&rdquo; can mean interrupted, the text doesn&rsquo;t portray an action being interrupted; indeed, the travel depicted in the scene continues despite the threat of the wolves outside the carriage. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-040-WIC$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$3905bc5f-99cb-48ed-85ce-e53fc1ec7865$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"45a109a3","external_id":"622d080b-68f5-454f-8cb0-2a9f3be0c3f6","vaultid":"3abdef0e-8c1b-4a61-b0f9-f14d9a40ac06","uId":"076d8000-b12c-403a-b1d9-200acb5cd538","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"WIC","skill_desc":"Words in Context","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":3,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>The following text is from Bram Stoker&rsquo;s 1897 novel <em>Dracula</em>. The narrator is being driven in a carriage through a remote region at night.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>The baying of the wolves sounded nearer and nearer, as though they were closing round on us from every side. I grew dreadfully afraid, and the horses shared my fear. The driver, however, was not in the least <span role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Referenced Content\"><u>disturbed</u></span>; he kept turning his head to left and right, but I could not see anything through the darkness.</p>\n</blockquote>","stem":"<p>As used in the text, what does the word &ldquo;disturbed&rdquo; most nearly mean?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"bcdb6b6b-d4e6-4c34-8628-9fb463ceecc1","content":"<p>Disorganized</p>"},{"id":"62943c2d-baeb-4873-b23a-07dda05d76d5","content":"<p>Alarmed</p>"},{"id":"6d7fc0bb-5c14-4215-b6b0-943f018199d5","content":"<p>Offended</p>"},{"id":"a36e6764-0ad8-40b8-a322-5a794b73e26d","content":"<p>Interrupted</p>"}],"keys":["62943c2d-baeb-4873-b23a-07dda05d76d5"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer because as used in the text, &ldquo;disturbed&rdquo; most nearly means alarmed. The text portrays the narrator traveling in a carriage as wolves howl in the surrounding darkness. The text contrasts the reaction of both the narrator and the horses pulling the carriage with that of the driver of the carriage: the narrator and horses are &ldquo;dreadfully afraid,&rdquo; but the driver is &ldquo;not in the least disturbed.&rdquo; In other words, the driver is not alarmed by the wolves nearby. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. Although in some contexts, &ldquo;disturbed&rdquo; can mean disorganized, the text doesn&rsquo;t portray a character acting in a disorganized manner; instead, the driver continues to drive the carriage, even though the horses pulling it are alarmed.&nbsp;Choice C is incorrect. Although in some contexts, &ldquo;disturbed&rdquo; can mean offended, the text doesn&rsquo;t portray one character feeling offended, or upset, by another&rsquo;s actions; instead, it contrasts the fear felt by the narrator with another character&rsquo;s lack of fear.&nbsp;Choice D is incorrect. Although in some contexts, &ldquo;disturbed&rdquo; can mean interrupted, the text doesn&rsquo;t portray an action being interrupted; indeed, the travel depicted in the scene continues despite the threat of the wolves outside the carriage. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-040-WIC","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"3905bc5f-99cb-48ed-85ce-e53fc1ec7865","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959322,"updateDate":1691007959322}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959322, 1691007959322),
    ($SATQ$47598085$SATQ$, $SATQ$2f8259a1-06bd-4edb-89f3-e51e05143294$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$489fe75e-39ab-48a6-b0fc-261f6e153627$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Craft and Structure$SATQ$, $SATQ$CAS$SATQ$, $SATQ$TSP$SATQ$, $SATQ$Text Structure and Purpose$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p><span role="region" aria-label="Referenced Content"><u>Yawn contagion occurs when one individual yawns in response to another&rsquo;s yawn.</u></span> Studies of this behavior in primates have focused on populations in captivity, but biologist Elisabetta Palagi and her colleagues have shown that it can occur in wild primate populations as well. In their study, which focused on a wild population of gelada monkeys (<em>Theropithecus gelada</em>) in Ethiopia, the researchers further reported that yawn contagion most commonly occurred in males and across different social groups instead of within a single social group.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice best describes the function of the first sentence in the text as a whole?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"62c70990-997d-4889-8ca1-7bf769eb0639","content":"<p>It defines a phenomenon that is discussed in the text.</p>"},{"id":"fdb4bc8b-c084-4eb3-b6c5-810bdac213d2","content":"<p>It introduces a problem that is examined in the text.</p>"},{"id":"30045779-9089-47d5-9203-2eb12ebfd55b","content":"<p>It makes a claim that is challenged in the text.</p>"},{"id":"45cc9e8d-e20e-42e1-bd58-039c034cd9f5","content":"<p>It presents a hypothesis that is evaluated in the text.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["62c70990-997d-4889-8ca1-7bf769eb0639"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer because it most accurately describes how the first sentence functions in the text as a whole. The first sentence introduces what yawn contagion is, explaining that it occurs when an individual yawns in response to the yawn of another individual. The text goes on to describe Elisabetta Palagi and her colleagues&rsquo; study of this phenomenon in a wild population of gelada monkeys. According to the text, the study showed that wild primate populations experience yawn contagion and that the behavior occurs most commonly in male monkeys and across social groups. Thus, the function of the first sentence is to define the phenomenon of yawn contagion that is discussed in the text. </p><p>Choice B is incorrect. Although the first sentence introduces the text&rsquo;s discussion of yawn contagion, it doesn&rsquo;t present this behavior, or anything else, as a problem. Choice C is incorrect because the first sentence doesn&rsquo;t present a claim but instead explains what yawn contagion is. Moreover, the text doesn&rsquo;t challenge anything; it&rsquo;s an informative text that describes the findings of a research study about yawning in wild primate populations. Choice D is incorrect. Although the text describes a scientific study, and most scientific studies are guided by a hypothesis, the text doesn&rsquo;t say what Palagi and her colleagues&rsquo; hypothesis was; the text discusses their findings instead. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-007-TSP$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$49b1a886-801d-4e1e-bccd-5ca035391893$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"47598085","external_id":"2f8259a1-06bd-4edb-89f3-e51e05143294","vaultid":"489fe75e-39ab-48a6-b0fc-261f6e153627","uId":"206213a4-9e14-4f92-ac95-ebae79522520","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"CAS","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill_cd":"TSP","skill_desc":"Text Structure and Purpose","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p><span role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Referenced Content\"><u>Yawn contagion occurs when one individual yawns in response to another&rsquo;s yawn.</u></span> Studies of this behavior in primates have focused on populations in captivity, but biologist Elisabetta Palagi and her colleagues have shown that it can occur in wild primate populations as well. In their study, which focused on a wild population of gelada monkeys (<em>Theropithecus gelada</em>) in Ethiopia, the researchers further reported that yawn contagion most commonly occurred in males and across different social groups instead of within a single social group.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice best describes the function of the first sentence in the text as a whole?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"62c70990-997d-4889-8ca1-7bf769eb0639","content":"<p>It defines a phenomenon that is discussed in the text.</p>"},{"id":"fdb4bc8b-c084-4eb3-b6c5-810bdac213d2","content":"<p>It introduces a problem that is examined in the text.</p>"},{"id":"30045779-9089-47d5-9203-2eb12ebfd55b","content":"<p>It makes a claim that is challenged in the text.</p>"},{"id":"45cc9e8d-e20e-42e1-bd58-039c034cd9f5","content":"<p>It presents a hypothesis that is evaluated in the text.</p>"}],"keys":["62c70990-997d-4889-8ca1-7bf769eb0639"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer because it most accurately describes how the first sentence functions in the text as a whole. The first sentence introduces what yawn contagion is, explaining that it occurs when an individual yawns in response to the yawn of another individual. The text goes on to describe Elisabetta Palagi and her colleagues&rsquo; study of this phenomenon in a wild population of gelada monkeys. According to the text, the study showed that wild primate populations experience yawn contagion and that the behavior occurs most commonly in male monkeys and across social groups. Thus, the function of the first sentence is to define the phenomenon of yawn contagion that is discussed in the text. </p><p>Choice B is incorrect. Although the first sentence introduces the text&rsquo;s discussion of yawn contagion, it doesn&rsquo;t present this behavior, or anything else, as a problem. Choice C is incorrect because the first sentence doesn&rsquo;t present a claim but instead explains what yawn contagion is. Moreover, the text doesn&rsquo;t challenge anything; it&rsquo;s an informative text that describes the findings of a research study about yawning in wild primate populations. Choice D is incorrect. Although the text describes a scientific study, and most scientific studies are guided by a hypothesis, the text doesn&rsquo;t say what Palagi and her colleagues&rsquo; hypothesis was; the text discusses their findings instead. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-007-TSP","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"49b1a886-801d-4e1e-bccd-5ca035391893","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959313,"updateDate":1691007959313}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959313, 1691007959313)
